Furthermore, OMS can give $\pi$ -interactions with olefins (formation of metal–olefin complexes) and feature a high adsorption enthalpy of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ which is advantageous for its gas sorption, and separation.52,93–97 In addition, OMS of electrondeficient metal atoms can act as Lewis acid sites, e.g. in catalytic reactions.32,39 The post-functionalization of OMS, in other words ligand substitution through first formation of an OMS followed by a new ligand coordination to the OMS in MOFs, can be used to postsynthetically functionalize MOFs in general.98
In this review, we focus on the synthetic strategies for the verified formation of OMS in MOFs and on applications where a strong evidence for the effect of OMS was provided. Moreover, we also summarize computational studies, because they are an important foundation for studying and predicting sorption properties and applications of OMS-MOFs. Therefore, this overview should help to advance the design of OMS-MOFs for desired applications.
2. Synthetic and structural overview on typical SBUs for open metal sites and coordinated solvent removal strategies
From the initial MOF synthesis, the (remaining) metal coordination sites and the MOF pores will be filled by the solvent which was used in the synthesis. This solvent will be a ligand at the metal atoms to reach their stable coordination number and act as a porogen for the formation of the framework structure. The stability of the SBU and thus the entire framework depends upon the coordination chemistry of the metal atom. A metal atom has a preferred or typical coordination geometry governed by coordination chemistry principles, such as the metal atom size and d-electron configuration (see above in Introduction). If the metal ions are not solely coordinated to the donor atoms of the bridging ligands, their coordination sphere is usually saturated with coordinating unidentate solvent molecules derived from the synthesis medium.
The as-synthesized MOF will have initially all metal ions in the SBU in their fully coordinatively saturated state, that is, most stable coordination environment according to their usual coordination numbers, which are typically six (octahedral) or four (tetrahedral) for most 3d transition metal ions. In order to obtain open metal sites in the metal SBUs of MOFs (Table 1) an initial labile terminal ligand which may typically be a solvent molecule needs to be removed from its coordinating metal atom (Fig. 2). The procedure for this ligand removal with OMS generation is usually solvent exchange and removal, which may be generally termed ‘‘activation’’68,96,99,100 and can be further differentiated into thermal, chemical101–104 or photothermal105 activation (Fig. 3).
The synthesis of OMS in MOFs by the general removal of coordinated solvent molecules must proceed without causing a collapse of the network structure.106,107 It should be noted, however that the salient features of MOFs, namely crystallinity and proven porosity must be retained upon OMS formation which is not always verified in the literature.
In general, the removal of these coordinated solvent molecules is accompanied by a change in the coordination geometry of the metal center.
The open metal sites in MOFs can be generated by the removal of coordinated labile solvent ligand molecules like water, alcohols (methanol and ethanol), acetonitrile, and DMF $(N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide). The attempt to simply remove the coordinated solvent ligand (and the solvent from the pores) by heating under vacuum, however, may often fail and result in framework degradation or complete decomposition. Some of the solvent ligands are low volatile (e.g. DMF), therefore require high temperature or long evacuation times. Others are more strongly coordinated to the network (e.g. water through stronger metal coordination or hydrogen-bonding to the ligand donor atoms) and have a high surface tension in their liquid state and may lead to cavitation effects, that is, network collapse upon their removal.108
In order to ensure network retention there are several strategies and procedures in the literature to remove the coordinated solvent molecules (Fig. 3):101–104,106
(I) Solvent exchange and removal by thermal activation.86,92 A less-volatile (higher boiling) solvent ligand is first exchanged with a more volatile (lower boiling) solvent. At the same time the less volatile solvent is thereby washed from the pores of the MOF. This solvent exchange procedure is then followed by thermal activation,83,98–100,109–116 that is, by applying heat energy and/or (high) vacuum.
(II) Chemical activation.101–104 A less-volatile solvent is first exchanged by a (washing) treatment with highly volatile and only very weakly coordinating halogenated solvents. Different from (I) the final halogenated solvent removal occurs upon air-drying without using extra thermal energy (accept the energy provided by room temperature). Thereby, the final drying procedure is simpler and faster than in (I).
(III) Photothermal activation.105 Especially for kinetically stable (inert) metal ions (e.g. $\mathbf{Cr}^{3+}$ ) solvent ligand exchange and thermal ligand exchange will be very slow. Instead irradiation with (UV-vis) light then leads to a fast ligand exchange or removal through the population of a photoactive excited state. Selected papers will be presented, which describe the removal of the coordinated solvent molecules to form open metal sites in their MOFs, by using these three synthetic strategies. Table 2 gives an overview of the OMS-MOFs with their removal strategies in detail, which are reported in this review.
In the following we only reference work where OMS formation was confirmed in combination with framework stability.
## 2.I Solvent exchange and removal by thermal activation
The most used strategy for forming OMS is by removing the coordinated solvent molecules from the metal atoms in MOFs by the solvent exchange (SE) method, followed by thermal activation (TA).86,87,93,98–100,109–114,116
Dinuclear paddle-wheel SBUs. The dinuclear paddle-wheel metal unit $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathbf{COO})_{4}(\mathrm{solvent})_{2}]$ is of great interest, because these structures can be systematically tuned by carboxylate linkers of different lengths and geometries.117–122 This unit is surrounded by four carboxylate groups in a 4-c square planar fashion (Fig. 4). The labile solvent molecules terminate the two axial positions of the metal handle.
For ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ the easy removal is due to the Jahn–Teller effect for the $\mathbf{d}^{9}.$ -ion which leads to weakly-bound apical ligands with long $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{L}$ bonds and a tendency towards formation of a squareplanar geometry.123–125
The first MOF, that was examined for open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, was HKUST-1, $\left[\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\right]$ . The framework is constructed of these dicopper paddle-wheel SBUs linked by trigonal 1,3,5-btc $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}\ =\ 1,3,5$ -benzentricarboxylate) linkers.31 During the activation step the color changed from pale blue to dark blue due to the loss of the water molecules coordinated to the Cu centers as the coordination number of the Cu atoms decreased from five (square-pyramidal) to four (square planar).126,127 Sometimes the dark-blue color of activated HKUST-1 is also referred to as deep-purple.105
Nair et al.93 reported on the SE method to activate the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites in HKUST-1 for olefin/paraffin separation. They analyzed the removal of the solvent molecules and the availability of open metal sites in detail by in situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), in situ mass spectrometry, and vaporphase binary breakthrough experiments. The Cu-bound water exchange was based on removal and replacement by acetone through washing three times with fresh dry acetone over two days. Acetone was finally removed by drying at $70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ and evacuating at room temperature (RT) for $24\mathrm{h}$ in a vacuum oven to yield the solvent-exchanged and thermally activated sample (sample S2).93
For comparison, an only thermally activated sample (sample S1) was prepared by drying at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and evacuating at RT for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in a vacuum oven at $7\times{{10}^{-4}}$ bar without previous SE. Nair et al. found that sample S2 had a $29\%$ higher BET (Brunauer–Emmett–Teller) surface area of $1600\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a pore volume of $0.71{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ than sample S1, which gave only $1240\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ with $0.55~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ pore volume.93
Fig. 5 shows the powder X-ray diffractions patterns (PXRD) of sample S1 and sample S2 at RT and the PXRDs at different temperatures of the thermally activated sample S1, where, the reflection at $6.5^{\circ}$ disappeared, which was seen as a proof for the removal of the aqua ligands on Cu2+.93
Furthermore, Nair et al. found that the total adsorption capacity and the equilibrium olefin selectivity in sample S2 was $44{-}55\%$ and $10\%$ higher, respectively, than in the thermally-only activated sample S1. The authors explained these results by using in situ FT-IR measurements to show that sample S2 has very few free carboxyl groups, hence, much less structure defects than sample S1. For the availability of OMS, they measured the amount of water desorbed from a known mass of completely dehydrated HKUST-1 and estimated the percentage of generated open metal sites (Fig. 6). Nair et al. found that the availability of OMS increases with the activation temperature, e.g., after activating at ${70~^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under a vacuum of $7\times{{10}^{-4}}$ bar, the percentage of open metal sites in sample S1 was $69\%$ . The fraction of $93\%$ of OMS could be made available by TA up to $230^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before the framework began to collapse. It was also concluded that the presence of defects in the material due to incomplete coordination of $\mathrm{btc}^{3-}$ linkers to the metal centers, led to stronger binding of the aqua ligands on the defective metal sites.93 The percentage of OMS in samples S2 was already $78\%$ after activating at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , $9\%$ higher than in sample S1 and increased further to $98\%$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .93
Hong et al.111 reported also on the activation of HKUST-1 at different temperatures. The as-synthesized HKUST-1 was dried at $378\mathrm{~K~}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum and activated in a nitrogen flow at different temperatures, 443, 473, 503, 523, and $553\ \mathrm{K},$ to remove the coordinated water molecules. Hong and his co-workers followed this removal using thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC). The first weight loss from room temperature to $378~\mathrm{~K~}$ of $10.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was due to the loss of physisorbed $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and residual ethanol in the MOF channels. The second weight loss of $4.3~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ from 378 and $473\mathrm{~K~}$ , and the third weight loss of $1.8~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ from 473 and $558~\mathrm{~K~}$ was eventually due to the dehydration of coordinated water in HKUST-1. The weight loss above $573\mathrm{~K~}$ represented the disintegration of the MOF structure. These experimental data were not in full agreement with the calculated mass change $(4.1~\mathrm{wt}\%\times2)$ for two coordinated water molecules. Instead the two desorption steps for coordinated water molecules suggested the presence of two non-equivalent coordinated water molecules on the paddlewheel unit. The authors also reported that the crystallite size and morphology of the as-synthesized and the all-activated HKUST-1 MOFs did not show any changes in the PXRD and of the morphology under the SEM (scanning electron microscope) until HKUST-1-553 K. From nitrogen adsorption measurements at 77 K the BET surface areas increased from 1382 (for HKUST-1- $443\mathrm{~\K~}$ over 1441 (for HKUST-1-503 K) to $1449\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (for HKUST-1-523 K). We critically note that these differences are still within experimental error of about $\pm50\mathrm{\m}^{2}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ and values of 1441 and $1449\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ can be considered identical.111 In 2016, Abbasi et al.109 prepared microbelts of HKUST-1 as a heterogeneous catalyst for selective organic sulfide oxidation. After synthesis, the resulting solid was washed with water and methanol and dried under vacuum between 120 and $150~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ Based on the TGA of these microbelts the removal of the coordinated water was assigned from the weight loss below 160 1C (Fig. 7).129,130
In activated HKUST-1 the presence and co-existence of $\mathrm{{Cu}^{1+}}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ was observed through the spectroscopic investigation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , CO or NO adsorbates (see in the application Section 4). Apparently, the oxidation state of copper can be reduced from $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}^{1+}}$ at higher temperature (approx. 473 K), for example by redox treatments, using vacuum and/or reducing gases at different sample temperatures.127,131–133
Other Cu MOFs that have been investigated for open metal sites are MOF-505 and MOF-2 $\left([\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{DMF})_{2}]\right)$ .86,98,134 Yaghi et al.86 reported on the Cu-MOF MOF-505, which is isoreticular to HKUST-1 with the $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\big(H_{4}b p t c\big)}$ ligand. Accordingly, the as-synthesized MOF-505 was activated by soaking in fresh acetone three times for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ each ( $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ total) to remove the DMF molecules from the pores. After that, the acetonecontaining material was carefully evacuated at $<1\times{10}^{-6}$ bar in three steps to remove the coordinated water molecules. In the first step, the solid kept at room temperature loses $42\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ over $^{15\mathrm{h}}$ and changes its color from blue-green to light blue. In the second step, at $70\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ for $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the weight decrease continues $\left(5.3~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ , and the color changes to dark blue. In the third step, the solid, kept at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , loses $4.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and changes its color to purple. Subsequently, the nitrogen uptake of MOF-505 increased over the three different activation steps. The nitrogen uptake in step I increased from $278~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $386~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in step II, with a final increase to $526~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in step III. The authors analyzed the stability of the framework under SE conditions using powder X-ray diffraction patterns and thermogravimetric analysis. They reported that the X-ray diffraction patterns of the simulated, as-synthesized, and acetone-exchanged scenarios showed nearly the same pattern and the PXRD of the desolvated material showed a decrease in the intensities and broader reflections with an additional broader reflection at around $7^{\circ}2$ -theta. The thermogravimetric analysis showed a weight loss of $30.8\%$ below $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , which agrees with the calculated value of $30.5\%$ from the liberation of 2.5 acetone and three water molecules per $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{b p t c}$ formula unit. Furthermore, they explained that the removal of noncoordinated DMF/water molecules and coordinated water molecules have two positive effects, due to a decrease of sample mass and an increase of available micropore volume and specific surface area, on the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. The OMS enhance the hydrogen-framework (SBU) interaction. The authors also reported that MOF-505 has a high hydrogen uptake capacity due to the availability of open metal sites. The hydrogen uptake, which is $14.1\mathrm{\mg\8^{-1}}$ $\left(1.41~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ after the first activation step, increased to $19.7\ \mathrm{mg\thinspaceg^{-1}}$ $(1.97~\mathrm{wt\%})$ in the second step, and finally to $24.7~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\left(2.47~\mathrm{wt\%}\right)$ after complete dehydration in the third step.86
Liu et al.98 reported on the Cu-bdc MOF MOF-2 as a heterogeneous interfacial catalyst for a sustainable Suzuki coupling reaction. For this, the open Cu sites were grafted with pyridylsalicylimine (Py-SI) to get Cu-bdc/Py-SI, and then palladium(II) chloride was added to produce Pd@Cu-bdc/Py-SI MOF. To remove the coordinated DMF solvent molecules, the SE and TA method was used. The as-synthesized Cu-bdc was washed one time with DMF, then with hot methanol $(70~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , filtered, dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, and activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum. The DMF-characteristic IR band at $1663~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ had disappeared after activation.98 Guo et al.110 investigated the Cu-MOF NEM-4 with open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in hydrocarbon storage and separation. Before measuring the gas sorption, NEM-4 was activated by the SE method using methanol and under dynamic vacuum at $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Hereby, the color of NEM-4 changed from pale blue to dark blue, similar to the situation of other Cu-MOFs in which open sites can be generated.110
The $\mathrm{d}^{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ ion (high-spin) also shows the Jahn–Teller effect and typically exhibits a tetragonally distorted-octahedral or squareplanar coordination environment. For example, $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ was activated by SE with methanol and dried under vacuum at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The fully activated framework exhibits a short $\mathbf{Cr-Cr}$ distance of $2.06(2)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ within the paddle-wheel SBU, which is typically interpreted as a quadruple bond.134 Consequently the $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ ion has a decreased Lewis acidity.135
Dinuclear distorted SBUs. For $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ the octahedral coordination is typical. Since the high-spin $\mathrm{d}^{5}{\cdot}\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ion has no crystal field stabilization energy, different geometries are possible depending on the coordinated ligands.136 Employing 5-(pyridin-3-yl)isophthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{{H_{2}p y i p}}\right)$ as a bifunctional trigonal linker in a solvothermal reaction with $\mathbf{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ leads to the construction of $[{\bf M}{\bf n}_{2}({\bf p}{\bf y i p})_{2}{\bf D}{\bf M}{\bf F}]$ DMFMeOH (NJU-Bai33; Nanjing University) including dinuclear distorted $[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}(\mathbf{COO})_{4}\mathbf{N}_{2}]$ SBUs.137 As shown in Fig. 8, two crystallographically independent $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ ions (Mn1 and Mn2) are present in the $[\bf M{n}_{2}(\bf C O O)_{4}N_{2}]$ SBU. The Mn1 coordination consists of five carboxylate moieties and one N atom from different linkers. The coordination environment of Mn2 is completed by an oxygen atom from DMF. Based on the thermogravimetric analysis the removal of the coordinated DMF molecules was achieved after SE with acetone followed by TA at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum.137
In 2012, Kitagawa et al.100 reported on the Cu-MOF $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)\big]{\cdot}D M F_{1.5},}$ obtained from $\mathbf{CuX}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\left(X\right)}~=~\mathbf{Cl}.$ $\mathbf{NO}_{3,}$ ) salts with 3,5-dimethyl-4-carboxypyrazole $\mathrm{\bf~\tilde{H}_{2}d m c a p z})$ . This compound consists of a dinuclear SBU were the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are surrounded by two N-donor atoms from two pyrazolate groups and by two carboxylate groups in a bidentate coordination mode each from different linkers. Further, the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are bridged by a $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH}_{2}$ water ligand achieving a distorted square-pyramidal geometry (Fig. 9). The presence of bridging water molecules in a MOF SBU is unusual.100
To obtain open metal sites they used a reversed combination of TA and SE method and described a stepwise removal. In the first step, the as-synthesized material $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)\big]{\cdot}D M F_{1.5}}$ was evacuated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{7\mathrm{h}}$ under high vacuum. The dark-blue TGA curve of $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)\big]{\cdot}D M F_{1.5}}$ in Fig. 10, left shows a weight loss of $30\%$ at around $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which stands for the removal of the DMF guest molecules and the aqua bridge, and a weight loss between 270 and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , due to framework decomposition. Then the sky blue $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{mcapz})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}_{0.9}$ was soaked in water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove remaining DMF solvent molecules together with impurities of unreacted ligand or $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ . In the next step, the water-exchanged pale blue $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)\big]{\cdot}H_{2}O_{4.5}}$ was evacuated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ under high vacuum. The pale blue TGA curve of $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)]{\cdot}H_{2}O_{4.5}}}$ in Fig. 10 shows a weight loss of $15\%$ at around $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , which means a loss of water guest molecules (calculated $16.1\%$ ), a small weight loss at around $120~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , due to the loss of one water molecule per $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ unit (experimental 3.8, calculated $4.3\%$ ) and a weight loss above $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ because of framework decomposition. The water removal is accompanied by a color change from pale blue to dark blue for which is akin to the color change in HKUST-1.31 Therefore, the color change was also evidence to the loss of the water molecules coordinated to the Cu centers. The coordination number of the Cu atoms should have decreased from five (distorted squarepyramidal geometry) to four (distorted square planar), albeit it can be noted that no structural proof on the material $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ was provided. The obtained dark-blue activated network $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]},$ returned its color to pale-blue within a few minutes under ambient moisture. Activated $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ adsorbed ambient moisture and returned into $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)\big]\cdot H_{2}O_{4.5}}.$ If after the final activation step, $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ was cooled to liquidnitrogen temperature (77 K, under He atmosphere) a forest green (proven porous) material $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ was obtained which was highly air- and moisture-stable at RT. The color change from dark blue to forest green at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ was interpreted to reflect a further change in the coordination of the metal centers. The authors assume a small twist of the dmcapz ligands concomitant with this change, so that a distortion from the initial square-planar geometry occurs to a distorted-tetrahedral coordination in $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{dmcapz})_{2}].$ The higher hydrophobicity of the forest-green material compared with the dark-blue one seen as due to the structural reorganization which took place during the freezing process. To return forest-green $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ into $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}(H_{2}O)]}\cdot H_{2}O_{4.5},}$ the former had to be soaked in deionized $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}.$ . The thermal stability of forest-green $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ was followed by TGA (Fig. 10) with the green TGA curve indicating a fully activated material.100
Differences in the PXRDs at various temperature reflected the structural changes at the activation step from $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{mcapz})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\mathrm{}.$ $\mathbf{DMF}_{1.5}$ to $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(d m c a p z)_{2}]}$ . From PXRD it was deduced that the m2-OH2 bridge molecule is removed between 90 and 110 1C.100
Trinuclear SBUs. The solvothermal reaction of ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}$ and $1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{dc}$ with the addition of imidazole as co-ligand $\mathrm{~L~}=\ 4{,}5$ -bis[4(4-pyridyl)phenyl]1-ethyl- $1H\cdot$ -imidazole), yielded a red-purple 3D-framework $\left[\mathrm{Co_{3}L(C H_{3}O H)_{0.5}(H_{2}O)_{0.5}(b d c)_{2.5}(O A c)}\right]$ solvent, which consists of $\mathbf{Co}_{3}$ -hourglass-like SBUs (Fig. 11).99 The desolvated form $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{L})(\mathrm{bdc})_{2.5}]$ with open ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites, was generated by heating at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . During the activation, one of the six-coordinated $\{\mathrm{CoO}_{5}\mathrm{N}\}$ chromophores changed to a coordinatively unsaturated five-coordinated one $\{\mathrm{CoO}_{4}\mathrm{N}\}$ . For the other three types of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centers, no coordination-mode change was observed. After removing the coordinated methanol molecules, the solvent accessible void volume in $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{L}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2.5}(\mathrm{OAc})]$ solvent increased to $1307\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , that is to $41\%$ of the total unit cell volume of 3181 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ . To regenerate $\left[\mathrm{Co_{3}L(C H_{3}O H)(b d c)_{2.5}(O A c)}\right]$ solvent without losing the single crystallinity, the activated form was soaked in methanol at room temperature for one day. Simulated and measured X-ray diffraction patterns were positively matched to further support the reversible methanol substitution-triggered single-crystal to single-crystal transformation. The thermogravimetric analysis of $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{L}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{0.5}\cdot$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{0.5}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2.5}(\mathrm{OAc})]$ solvent showed a weight loss of $4.8\%$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which indicates the removal of the $\mathbf{MeOH}/\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules. Based on TGA the desolvated OMS-containing $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\mathbf{\Tilde{\tau}}\mathbf{MOF}$ was stable up to 350 1C.99
It has to be noted that the coordination chemistry of ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ is a special case with its easy and reversible octahedral to tetrahedral transition. When two labile solvent ligands are present, then upon their removal the octahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ environment changes to a tetrahedral ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ geometry, which can be identified through the concomitant color change from pink (octahedral) to blue (tetrahedral).116 However, this well-known transition does not form OMS. Therefore, examples of such Co-MOFs were not included here as OMS-MOFs. It is also known that ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ forms low-spin compounds with strong-field ligands, which are often planar.
Open ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\sec^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{V}^{3+/4+}$ and site MOFs with trinuclear $\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})_{6}$ SBUs have been investigated in the 3D terephthalate (bdc) based structures MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-101(V) and in the trimesate (btc) based network $\mathbf{MIL-}100(\mathrm{Cr})$ $\mathbf{\hat{MIL}}=\mathbf{\hat{\Phi}}$ Materiaux de l’Institute Lavoisier). These MIL frameworks have the empirical formulae $\left[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathbf{O})\mathbf{X}(\mathrm{bdc}_{3}/\mathrm{btc}_{2})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\right]$ ( $\mathbf{\M}=\mathbf{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , $\sec^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{V}^{3+/4+}$ $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{OH}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{{F}^{-}}$ ). $^{112,113,138-142}$ Both MIL-100 and MIL-101 consist of three $\upmu_{3}$ -oxido vertex-sharing $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ octahedra, which are interconnected by $\operatorname{bdc}^{2-}$ or $\mathrm{btc}^{3-}$ ligands.12 The trinuclear SBU $\left[\mathrm{M}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{\bf O})(\mathrm{F/OH})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}^{-})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ in MIL-101 and MIL-100 compounds has two terminal aqua ligands at two of the three octahedral $\mathbf{M}^{3+}$ ions (Fig. 12). The octahedral environment of the third metal atom is achieved through the coordination of an anionic ligand $\left(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\right.$ or $\mathbf{F}^{-}$ , depending on the synthesis.113,143–149
In 2018, Mohsenzad et al.112 reported on $\mathbf{MoO(O_{2})_{2}@E n/M I L}.$ $100\mathrm{(Cr)}$ as a new bifunctional $\mathbf{Mo/Cr-MOF}$ catalyst for selective thioether oxidation. To generate open metal sites, MIL-100(Cr) was activated in hot DMF at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , or in ethanol at $60~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , or in water at $90~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , then it was dried at room temperature (for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ ) and under vacuum between 120 and $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .138 By comparison, MIL-100(Fe) was activated in hot water at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 hours and then in hot ethanol at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{h}$ . After drying at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (overnight) and under vacuum the two aqua ligands were removed to form open $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites (see dehydration, Fig. 13). When the sample was dried above $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(12\mathrm{~h~})$ and under vacuum increasing amounts of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ were observed by M¨oßbauer spectroscopy which reach $14\mathrm{mol}(\mathrm{Fe})\%$ at $260^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ degassing for $^{90\mathrm{h}}$ . The formation of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ is due to the removal of the anionic ligands $\mathrm{\oH^{-}}$ or $\mathbf{F}^{-}$ (see partial reduction, Fig. 13).142
Several groups reported on MIL-101(Cr) for its catalysts, gas sorption, postsynthetic modification etc. In order to obtain OMS in activated MIL-101(Cr) the material is typically heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for one day (24 h).113,143–154
Tetranuclear SBUs. A series of isostructural tetrazolate MOFs with the chemical formula $\mathrm{{\bfM}_{3}[(M_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}(s o l v e n t)_{12}]_{2}}$ , named
M-btt $(\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ $\begin{array}{r c l}{\displaystyle\mathtt{b t t}^{3-}}&{=}&{1,3,5}\end{array}$ -benzenetristetrazolate) have been discussed in accordance to the presence of a high density of OMS.155–158 A representative structure of the SBU in M-btt is given in Fig. 14. In general, these SBUs consist of chloride-centered square-planar $\mathrm{[{\bfM}_{4}C l]^{7+}}$ units, which are connected by eight triangular $\mathrm{btt}^{3-}$ bridging ligands. The sixth coordination site on each $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ ion is terminated by aqua ligands. These terminal aqua ligands can be removed through SE followed by TA to achieve OMS, verified with PXRD and TGA.157–160 For example, Fe-btt, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}[(\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{12}]_{2};$ was washed with methanol and activated by heating at $135^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under a dynamic vacuum. Cr-btt, $\mathrm{Cr_{3}[(C r_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}(D M F)_{12}]_{2}}$ was activated by soaking in fresh DMF, then washed with methanol and finally heating at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under a dynamic vacuum. Cu-btt, $\mathrm{{HCu}[(C u_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}(H_{2}O)_{12}],}$ was activated by a complete exchange of DMF for methanol and heating at $120~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for approx. $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under reduced pressure. Mn-btt, $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{12}]_{2}$ , was washed with DMF and methanol several times and activated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under dynamic vacuum.157–160
However, Queen et al.156 showed that upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading a rapid drop in the isosteric heat of adsorption in Fe- and Mn-btt occurs, because some of the primary metal adsorption sites are still blocked by solvent molecules. Based on Rietveld refinement of the neutron diffraction data, the authors explained that the activated OMS M-btt frameworks showed that $23\%$ (Cr), $65\%$ (Fe) and $17\%$ (Cu) of the metal sites (as potential OMS) were still occupied by coordinated solvent. For identically activated M-btt, the order for the amount of available OMS is $\mathbf{Cu}\approx\mathbf{Cr}>\mathbf{Fe}>\mathbf{Mn}$ . In this study the M-btt MOFs had been activated by the sequence soaking in DMF at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , decanting, soaking three times in (fresh) methanol at $343\mathrm{~K~}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , filtration, and heating under dynamic vacuum $\scriptstyle(<10\upmu\mathrm{bar})$ at 433 K for 24 h.156
Also, earlier in situ studies of ${\bf D}_{2}$ adsorption yielded a significant number of metal sites in these MOFs that are still blocked by solvent molecules which had not been removed by normal activation procedures.157,160 We note that these works clearly demonstrate that quantitative OMS generation cannot be taken for granted by ‘‘standard’’ activation protocols. It must be noted that more studies are needed to quantify the formed OMS.157,160
Hexanuclear SBUs. The class of UiO (University in Oslo) 3D frameworks consist of hexanuclear $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{\bf-O}_{4})(\upmu_{3}{\bf-O}\mathrm{H})_{4}\right]$ clusters with eight-coordinated square-antiprismatic zirconium ions (Fig. 15, left). The prototypical UiO-66 has the formula unit $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b t c)_{6}]}}$ .161 The synthesis of open $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ sites can be described with a gradual removal of two water molecules upon TA (Fig. 15). The loss of the first water molecule leads to a reduction in coordination number from eight to seven of three $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ ions within the cluster. The lost water molecules originate from and are composed of a $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ group together with the H-atom of another $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ group. The removal of the second water molecule lowers the coordination number of two more $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ ions to seven and one $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ from seven to six.105,114,162–164 Lamberti et al.165 reported on a study of the hydroxylated and dehydroxylated forms of UiO-66. They used the TA method to remove the solvent molecules from the ordered $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}]}$ cluster and to generate a distorted $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}$ cluster. Based on the thermogravimetric analysis the loss of two water molecules per $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}]}$ cluster were obtained after heating above $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(300~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ . IR data confirmed the full removal of OH groups and EXAFS (extended X-ray absorption fine structure) confirmed the loss of about one oxygen atom in the first coordination sphere of the Zr atoms.165
Gao et al.114 reported also on the dehydration of UiO-66. They explained that the dehydrated UiO-66 with open Lewisacid sites is a heterogeneous and size-selective catalyst for cyanosilylation reaction of aldehydes with trimethylsilylcyanide. To obtain the dehydrated UiO-66 with OMS ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4^{+}}$ , hydrous UiO-66 was calcinated at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for only two hours.114
In 2018, Speybroeck et al.162 reported on the intrinsic dynamic nature of UiO-66. The structural deformation of
UiO-66 can be explained through defect formation or removal of coordinated molecules. In their work UiO-66 was dehydrated between 523 and $573\mathrm{~K~}$ .162
Infinite rod-shaped SBUs. The ligand para-2,5-dioxido-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate $\left({\mathrm{dobdc}}^{4-}\right)$ with the metals $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ leads to the formation of the isostructural series M-MOF-74, $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(p\mathbf{-}\mathbf{dobdc})(\mathbf{solvent})_{2}]$ (also known as CPO-27-M, solvent ${\bf\Pi}={\bf H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ or DMF) which have infinite rod-shaped SBUs.167–176 Also the ligand 4,6-dioxido-1,3- benzene-dicarboxylate $\left(m{\cdot}\operatorname{dobdc}^{4-}\right)$ leads to the same topology in $\left[\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{2}(m\mathrm{-dobdc})(\mathrm{solvent})_{2}\right]$ as a structural isomer to the M-MOF-74 series.88 In M-MOF-74, edge connected $\{\mathbf{MO}_{6}\}$ octahedra infinite linked rod shape SBUs with one coordinated water molecule per metal center (Fig. 16). The coordinated water or DMF molecule completes the octahedral coordination environment of each metal center. The bridging linkers then combine these parallel SBUs to give hexagonal, one-dimensional channels.
The OMS of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ sites in the M-MOF-74 series can be generated by using SE followed by TA. Through desolvation of the coordinated solvent molecule the metal center converts to a distorted square pyramidal coordination environment with the OMS pointing into the channel. $^{70,88,101-105}$ The originally six-coordinated metal ions $\mathbf{d}^{7}{\cdot}\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\left(\mathrm{high\spin}\right)$ and $\mathsf{d}^{9}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ feature the Jahn–Teller distortion. It should be noted that the pseudo-octahedral coordination environment of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ show an elongation along the axial bonds and this allows for an easier removal of the axially coordinated solvent molecule. At the same time the Jahn–Teller effect, impedes the approach and strong binding of molecules on the open metal site.70,177
Moreover, Moon et al.178 reported on $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})(\mathbf{EG})_{2}]$ with coordinated ethylene glycol molecules (EG). To generate open $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ and ${\bf N i}^{2+}$ sites, the single crystals were soaked in anhydrous methanol and heated up to $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Based on singlecrystal X-ray analysis (SCXRD) the coordinated and free EG molecules were replaced with methanol and based on the thermogravimetric analysis the coordinated methanol molecule was completely removed up to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Lobkovsky et al.180 reported an ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ MOF also with an infinite rod-shaped SBU, in which the OMS can be generated for the sensing of small molecules. $\big[\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})\big]$ is isostructural with MOF-76 and was achieved in a solvothermal reaction $\mathrm{Eu}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{3}$ btc in a solvent mixture of DMF, ethanol, and water.35 As shown in Fig. 17 the rod-shaped SBU consists of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions coordinated by six carboxylate oxygen atoms from the $\mathrm{btc}^{3-}$ linker and an oxygen atom from one water molecule in a seventh site. The as-synthesized $\mathrm{\small{[Eu(btc)(H_{2}O)]\cdot1.5H_{2}O}}$ was activated at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for one day and the removal of the free and Eu-bound water molecules was assessed by TGA where the water molecules were removed in the temperature range of 25 to 150 1C.179
The solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with a T-shaped tetrazolyl-carboxyl ligand $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{tzpa}$ (5-(4-(tetrazol-5-yl)phenyl)isophthalic acid) leads to the formation of the MOF $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{tzpa})\cdot$ $(\mathbf{OH})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathbf{DMF}$ based on infinite ribbon-like SBUs.181 Which can be thought of being constructed from edge-sharing $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\big(\mathrm{N}_{4}\mathrm{C}\big)_{3}\big(\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH}\big)$ -metallacycles. As shown in Fig. 18, two of the three Co atoms have two aqua ligands each. The removal of these coordinated water molecules was achieved through SE with methanol followed by TA at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum, to yield the solvent-free framework $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{tzpa})(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})\right]$ .181
The group of Dinc˘a et al.83 demonstrated that the replacement of carboxylate moieties with triazolate ligands leads to the formation of networks with similar topology to the architecture of M-MOF-74. The solvothermal reaction of a bisbenzotriazole ligand, bis(1H-1,2,3-triazolo[4,5-b], $,[4^{\prime},5^{\prime}-i])$ dibenzo[1,4]dioxin $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{btdd}\right)$ with hydrated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ chlorides leads to the formation of the three isostructural MOFs, $\begin{array}{r}{[{\bf M}{\bf n}_{2}{\bf C}\mathrm{l}_{2}({\bf b}{\bf t}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{d})({\bf H}_{2}{\bf O})_{2}],}\end{array}$ $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{btdd})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ and $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{btdd})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ . The octahedral coordination environment of the metal center was built by the coordination of three N-donor atoms from different btdd2 linkers, two bridging chloride ions and one terminal water molecule pointing into the channel (Fig. 19). The authors used Soxhlet extraction with methanol to remove the solvent molecules in the as-synthesized materials and then continued with TA ( $\mathrm{100~^{\circ}C}$ under dynamic vacuum) to create open $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ and ${\bf N i}^{2+}$ sites. They explained that upon activation, the color changed from red to green $[\mathbf{Co}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{btdd})]$ and from white to lime-green $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{btdd})].$ due to the change in the metal-ion coordination number from six to five, while $\left[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{btdd})\right]$ remained off-white.83 The MAF-X27 $\mathbf{\left(M\right)}=\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\mathbf{\vec{\mu}},$ 1 and MAF-X25 $\bf\left(M_{\alpha}=M n^{2+\alpha}$ ) MOFs with the $^{1H,5H\cdot}$ -benzene(1,2- $d{:}4,5{-}d^{\prime}.$ bistriazole $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bbta}\right)$ ligand have the same SBU as [M2Cl2(btdd)] (cf. Fig. 19).182–184
Open metal sites can be also obtained by using a so-called urothermal synthesis.185 Hereby, various urea derivatives were used as solvents, which can be bound reversible to the metal sites, act as porogen, apparently aid in the crystallization and can be removed by SE to generate porosity and potential OMS. For example, the ${\bf Y}^{3+}$ sites in as-synthesized crystals in $[\mathrm{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}(\mathrm{e}{\cdot}\mathrm{urea})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ are eight-coordinate, of which there are two urea solvent molecules for potential OMS.185
## 2.II Chemical activation
Chemical activation (CA) is a variant of ‘‘solvent exchange and removal by thermal activation’’ aims to allow for the final solvent removal at room temperature (RT), that is, without additional heating.
It can be noted that supercritical drying with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $\left(\operatorname{scco}_{2}\right)$ 1 would also belong to CA, since a stronger coordinating solvent together with the pore solvent would be replaced by $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ which is subsequently removed at or below RT. The supercritical carbon dioxide activation, which is performed by exchanging acetone or ethanol molecules, corresponds to the CA with halogenated solvents (see below), but is a much ‘‘greener’’ route, due to the use of the non-halogenated green solvent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .186,187 Upon $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ removal no (or little) surface tension or cavitation effects are expected – different to the removal of more strongly coordinating solvents. Freeze-drying is another solvent removal process, where the products are dried at temperatures below the freezing point of the solvent. This process has a direct solid-to-gas phase transition (sublimation), without the liquid phase.188 Thus, both CA (including $\mathbf{scCO}_{2}$ drying) and freeze-drying aim to prevent the framework collapse in the case of less-stable MOF structures. The only examples of the use of $\mathbf{scCO}_{2}$ in connection with OMS reported a case with framework collapse.189,190 Cases where $\mathbf{scCO}_{2}$ or freeze-drying were used for the simple solvent removal from the pores, without dedicated OMS formation, are of course numerous.191–194
The mild CA by chlorinated solvents was described in detail for the activation of HKUST-1, but was also reported for MOF-2 and examples of M-MOF-74.101–103 Jeong et al.101–104 reported the CA route by treatment with halogenated solvents, like dichloromethane (DCM), without using extra thermal energy to remove the introduced halogenated solvent molecules in HKUST-1, MOF-2, M-MOF-74 $\mathbf{\hat{M}}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Ni). In contrast to the conventional TA route which is performed by applying heat energy to above room temperature and vacuum (possibly after SE), the CA proceeds by soaking the MOFs in halogenated solvents several times at room temperature and in a moisture-free argon-charged glove box.101–103
Fig. 20 shows schematically the CA route in ways of direct and multiple coordination exchange. The first step of the direct coordination exchange way is the exchange of DMF with DCM, and the second step is the spontaneous dissociation of the very weakly coordinated DCM at RT. As a variant, the multiple coordination exchange way proceeds by first exchanging the bound low-volatile DMF solvent with a more volatile one, such as MeCN, MeOH or EtOH followed by the final exchange with DCM.101–104
HKUST-1 was used as a proof of principle for the CA methodology. After synthesis, an activated solvent-free sample of HKUST-1 was prepared and loaded with DMF to prove the removal of the low-volatile DMF solvent through this method. The DMF coordinated and loaded HKUST-1 was soaked 30 times in DCM $30\times10~\mathrm{min}$ , RT) (direct exchange pathway, Fig. 20). The subsequent solution NMR analysis of the digested HKUST-1 showed no more signal of DMF (Fig. 21). Alternatively, the DMF-coordinated and -loaded HKUST-1 was soaked in MeOH, EtOH or MeCN (several times $\times10\mathrm{min}$ , RT). Then the obtained MeOH, EtOH or MeCN coordinated HKUST-1 was soaked 30 times in DCM ( $30\times10$ min, RT) (multiple exchange pathway, Fig. 20). Again, the solution NMR analysis revealed the complete solvent removal (Fig. 21). Thereby, it is also shown that methanol, ethanol or acetonitrile can be removed by multiple soaking with DCM.101–104
## 2.III Photothermal activation
In comparison to the above methods of solvent exchange and removal and chemical activation, which are performed by using solvents (and heat energy), the photothermal activation (PA) proceeds by only irradiation of the solid MOF sample. In 2018, Maspoch et al.105 reported on this one-step activation method for MOFs by using UV-vis irradiation of $300{-}650~\mathrm{\nm}$ . The authors suggested that the light-to-heat conversion has significant potential in evaporation processes because of the local nature of the heat generated, thereby minimizing heat diffusion and energy loss. Under direct irradiation the MOFs experience a significant temperature increase (to $120{-}220\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ in a few minutes $\mathrm{\Lambda}^{\prime}{\le}30\mathrm{\min})$ at atmospheric pressure, so that solvent molecules can be removed, and open metal sites can be generated. The MOFs exhibit absorption bands in the range $300{-}650\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The authors also pointed out that the MOF-tolight guide distance, ‘‘the distance of lamp to sample’’, is an important factor that can influence the PA of MOFs. The authors used several verification methods, such as PXRD, ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption, UV-vis spectroscopy to analyze the successful activation.105
Evaluation of the activation parameters for HKUST-1, showed that the light distance and concomitant irradiance with the resulting temperature are crucial. A shorter distance induced a higher temperature. For example, when HKUST-1 was directly irradiated at $3\ \mathrm{cm}$ ( $2650\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ , $5~\mathrm{min}$ , $\sim250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , it turned amorphous and nonporous, and when it was irradiated at $5\ \mathrm{cm}$ $(900\mathrm{\mW}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $5~\mathrm{min}$ , $\sim187\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , it remained crystalline and porous. Furthermore, they reported that the BET surface area increased from $1209\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{(7~cm,500~mw~cm^{-2}}$ , $5~\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ), over 1583 $7\ \mathrm{cm}$ , $500\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ , $30\ \mathrm{min})$ to $1819\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (5 cm, $900\ \mathrm{mW}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ ). In comparison, the BET surface area of the conventionally activated HKUST-1 (through SE and TA) was between 1450 to $1800\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .105
Fig. 22 shows that the color of the HKUST-1 powder changed from pale blue to dark blue (deep purple) after irradiation with the UV-vis lamp. The color change signaled the loss of the water molecules coordinated to the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel clusters. The authors explained that the solid-state UV-vis spectrum of HKUST-1 showed a broad absorption band at $720~\mathrm{nm}$ attributed to the d–d transition of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion. The contribution of this absorption band was further studied by irradiating HKUST-1 in wavelength ranges of $320\mathrm{-}390~\mathrm{nm}$ (UV) and of $390{\mathrm{-}}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ (visible). As expected, in both cases, lower temperatures (40 and $60\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , respectively) were reached, demonstrating that the irradiation wavelengths close to the absorption maximum contributes significantly to the photothermal effect.105 Using this PA process, Maspoch and co-workers105 activated also $\mathrm{Zn/Ni/Mg{\mathrm{-}}M O F{\mathrm{-}}74}$ , by following conditions, $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\colon145^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ , $30\mathrm{min}$ ; Ni: $167^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 30 min; and Mg: $136^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $30\mathrm{min}$ . Also, UiO-66, by these conditions: $57\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $30~\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.IV Quantifying OMS
The techniques gas or vapor adsorption, probe molecule infrared spectroscopy and temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) were used to quantify the relative amount (percentage) of Lewis acid sites or OMS in MOFs.195,196
OMS should be the primary adsorption site for suitable adsorbate molecules, such as $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and CO with significantly higher heats of adsorption than for the remaining pore walls. Thus, if the completed adsorption of such gas or vapor molecules to OMS identifies themselves through a step in the adsorption isotherm before adsorption continues at the pore walls, then the absolute gas uptake allows to quantify the amount of OMS in the given sample mass. Also, the step in the heat of adsorption curve versus the amount adsorbed can be used to quantify the OMS. Examples are $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ isosteric heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ curves plotted against $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal (see Section 4, $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption and Fig. 35, 37, 38 and 39, left). The isosteric heat of adsorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ is usually obtained using the Clausius–Clapeyron relation from $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ isotherm data at 77 and $87\mathrm{~K~}$ which are fitted with appropriate isotherm equation, e.g., a dual-site Langmuir–Freundlich equation or a trisite Langmuir equation.88
IR spectroscopy quantifies OMS through the increasing or decreasing absorbance of bands of adsorbed or desorbed probe molecules at the metal atoms. Used probe molecules are for example water, pyridine, deuterated acetonitrile, acetone and CO.195,197,198
TPD can identify the type and amount of Lewis-acid sites through the chemisorption of a chosen molecular probe at a low temperature followed by measuring the rate of its desorption as the temperature increases.195 TPD of ammonia11,199–204 and nitrogen oxide $\left(\mathrm{NO}\right)^{205}$ were, for example, used to probe Lewisacid sites. It has been pointed out, however, that the use of ammonia TPD in estimating MOF OMS densities presents two major drawbacks: First, the temperature range for desorption from different types of acid sites can often overlap, making peak deconvolution a necessary step in quantifying these different acid sites. Second, the temperatures required to desorb strong basic probe molecules such as ammonia may exceed the MOF thermal stability limit.206 The methods of vapor adsorption and probe molecule IR spectroscopy are briefly explained with the following illustrative examples. Bollini et al.206 used the quantity of irreversibly water adsorbed at room temperature on MIL-100(Cr) to measure the OMS density under a given set of activation conditions. Given the chemical binding of water molecules in the first coordination sphere of the metal as aqua ligands, it was assumed that adsorption of water onto open-metal sites at room temperature is irreversible under not too harsh activation condition. The type V adsorption isotherms of water onto the MOF exhibits three distinct steep regions (Fig. 23). Region I lies between approximately 0 and $1\times{10}^{-4}$ bar and the water uptake corresponds roughly to 1 mol of water per mole chromium and the authors suggested that adsorption in this region of the isotherm may occur exclusively on open chromium sites (obtained when activated at 523 K).
The calculated open-metal site count close to a value of 1 mol $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ per mol $\mathrm{cr}$ was interpreted in that $523\mathrm{~K~}$ is a sufficiently high temperature under vacuum to render the vast majority (if not all) of the chromium in the MIL-100(Cr) material under investigation as open-metal chromium.206 However, we note that an irreversible water uptake of 1 mol $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(\mathrm{mol\cr})^{-1}$ cannot be rationalized with the empirical formula $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{O})\mathrm{X}(\mathrm{btc}_{2})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ 1 $\mathbf{\tilde{X}}=\mathbf{OH}^{-}$ or $\mathbf{F}^{-}$ ) of MIL-100(Cr) which has only 2 mol $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(3\mathrm{\mol\cr})^{-1}$ or 0.66 mol $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $\left(\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{Cr}\right)^{-1}$ .141
Still, increasing the activation temperature in MIL-100(Cr) from 303 to $523\mathrm{~K~}$ (at a given vacuum and time) clearly showed an increasing fraction of OMS (Fig. 24, left).206
In parallel, the relative infrared absorbance of hydroxyl vibrations was used for the quantification of OMS with increasing temperature. Aqua ligands at chromium in MIL100(Cr) gave bands at 3672 and $3649~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The areas for the aqua-ligand hydroxyl vibrations decrease upon activation between $303\substack{-523\mathrm{~K~}}$ . The relative quantity of remaining coordinated water species was estimated by comparing the total area of the $\nu(\mathrm{OH})$ bands at 3672 and $3649~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for each activation temperature to that at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ , which in turn gave an estimate for the fraction of coordinated water removed (Fig. 24, right). We note that the equation for the estimation should be $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}$ retained $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ area $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{Cr}\right)_{T=x}/\mathrm{a}\mathrm{1}$ ea $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\right)_{T=303\mathbf{K}}$ or $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}{\cdot}\cdots\mathbf{Cr}$ removed $=1$  area $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{Cr}\right)_{T=x}/\mathrm{are}$ a $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\right)_{T}=303\ \mathbf{K}$ where $x$ is the temperature at which MIL-100(Cr) has been evacuated at for 1 h.206
## 3. Computational studies
The focus of MOF synthesis lies on the design of MOFs with binding sites that are tuned to the adsorption of a preferred guest molecule. MOFs with OMS contain such stronger binding sites which lead to an increased interaction with different sorbate molecules (see Section 4), compared to MOFs where the metal sites are fully occupied. The knowledge, how various OMS interact with the guest molecules opens access to new materials that can be tailored to a desired property.207 It should be noted, however, that these interactions cannot easily be deduced from experiments. Computational studies can help in these cases and are a main foundation for studying and predicting sorption and separation properties in MOFs.
For the computational description of MOFs, a variety of different approaches exist, which differ in computational demand and accuracy.208 Force-field methods form a cost-efficient way to reproduce structural properties of periodic MOFs and, depending on their parametrization, allow for a fairly accurate prediction of geometry parameters. Simulations using standard force fields, however, often fail in reproducing the interaction of the sorbate molecules and OMS.171,209–211 Here, an adjustment of those parameters which include adsorbate/OMS interactions is necessary to obtain a quantitative prediction of adsorption isotherms. Force fields derived from quantum mechanical (QM) calculations allow for a much better description of the interaction between guest molecules and OMS.171,211 Thermodynamic data, as e.g. adsorption isotherms, is accessible through Grand-Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations based on classical and QM-improved force fields.212–219
Density-functional theory (DFT) calculations have been most widely applied to predict the electrostatic interactions between the adsorbate molecules with OMS. The accuracy of the DFT method, however, depends on the employed exchange correlation functional.220 Generally, most of the exchange–correlation functionals that neglect dispersion effects fail in describing the adsorbate/OMS interaction energy.221,222 Here, pure generalized gradient approximation (GGA) functionals (e.g. PBE) and hybrid functionals (B3LYP, oB97X)211,220,222,223 mostly underestimate the dispersive interaction, while local density approximation (LDA) functionals222 overestimate them, due to their inability to describe long-range electron correlation, which is responsible for van der Waals (vdW) or London dispersion forces.221,223–228
The inclusion of dispersion corrections has been proven to be crucial for improving the accuracy of the calculated results. To capture weak interactions and improve the accuracy in simulations, the implementation of vdW density and dispersion corrected functionals have been proposed.70 These can be used in combination with the standard exchange–correlation functionals.
An approach to describe the dispersion forces by vdW empirical corrections has been introduced by Grimme et al. and the associated corrections are denoted as ‘‘-D’’ functionals.224 Depending on the level of dispersion correction, the extended functionals are referred to as -D1,229 - $.\mathrm{D}2^{224}$ and -D3.230 Quantum mechanics (QM) methods based on ab initio wave function methods such as the Møller–Plesset Second-Order (MP2) level of theory and the coupled cluster (CC) approach have also been applied to study the gas adsorption on OMS.222,231 In order to improve the accuracy of adsorption energies, a promising approach was found in combined QM/QM and QM/MM methods.221,222,232,233 Nachtigall et al. showed that improved results can be achieved by employing a combined DFT-ab initio DFT/CC computational method.232 Here, periodic DFT calculations with the PBE exchange correlation functional were employed to model the periodic crystal, while a subset of the structure (i.e. an embedded cluster) was described with the coupled cluster (CC) method including singles and doubles with perturbative triples (CCSD(T)).
Gas adsorption on OMS has been simulated for various gases using Grand-canonical Monte Carlo, DFT and ab initio methods of electronic structure. These studies have been employed to determine various sorption sites within the MOFs and to elucidate the role of the OMS related to the electronic interactions with different guest molecules. Using cluster models of HKUST-1 and a highly accurate coupled-cluster singles doubles (CCSD) approach, Rubesˇ et al. predicted that the OMS/adsorbate interactions can be classified into dispersion-driven interactions $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , electrostatic and dispersion interactions $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathbf{H}_{2},\mathbf{N}_{2},}\end{array}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ ), and the interaction with a partially covalent (dative) bonding character (CO).221
In this part, we consider computations on OMS-MOFs with gas and vapor adsorbates.221 Hereby, we focus on the accuracy of the selected computational methods referring to the binding enthalpies in comparison to experimental data. It should be noted that some authors give the calculated binding energies $(\Delta E)$ as electronic energies. These cannot be directly compared with the experimental heats of adsorption $\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}},~\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)$ . To achieve a comparable value, the calculated electronic binding energies need to be adjusted by adding finite temperature and zero-point energy (ZPE) effects to approach the binding enthalpy $(\Delta H)$ .70,222,223,234,235 However, both the predicted $\Delta E$ and $\Delta H$ results provide information on how the adsorbate/OMS interaction can be tuned by metal substitution, thereby producing novel promising adsorbents for a particular adsorbate.
## Computational adsorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ on OMS
In 2014, Space et al. performed GCMC simulations of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in Mg-MOF-74 using the three-site Darkrim– Levesque $\left(\mathrm{DL}\right)^{236}$ model (model 1) and the five-site charge– quadrupole Belof Stern Space $\left(\mathrm{BSS}\right)^{2.37}$ model (model 2).238 Both models include charge–quadrupole interactions, but no explicit terms to describe polarizability. Compared to the experimentally obtained $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherm for Mg-MOF-74 at lower pressures $\mathrm{\left(<10\mathrm{kPa}\right)}$ , these models show a significantly lower $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake and do not reproduce the sharp increase at lower pressures (Fig. 25, left). This and the calculated initial isosteric heats of adsorption of $-6.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ and $-6.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ indicate that these models do not capture the sorption on $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ sites (Fig. 25, right). The total energy in these simulations was dominated by van der Waals contributions, hence sorption at open metal sites was not detected.238
Space et al. showed that the BSSP model237 (model 3) which includes explicit many-body polarization interactions, proves most suitable for an accurate reproduction of the experimental $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms and the resulting $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values for M-MOF-74 and the rht type MOFs, PCN-61 and NOTT-112.238–240 In NOTT-112, two distinct environments for the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ metal sites within the paddle-wheel SBU were determined.240 There are ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions which face the cuboctahedral cage of the network and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions which are directed towards the linker. Simulations using the BSSP model indicated that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions which face to the cuboctahedral cage of the network have a higher partial positive charge. Hence, these ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions represent the main $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption sites.240
For Mg-MOF-74, the BSSP model produces an isotherm with a sharp increase at lower pressures $\left(<5\mathrm{{kPa}}\right)$ , which is also seen in the experiment (Fig. 25, left).238
Also, the calculated value for the heat of adsorption $\left(-10.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ is very close to the experiment $\left(-11.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ , indicating the sorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules on the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ OMS (Fig. 25, right and Table S2 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). Furthermore, a $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ center distance of 2.60 Å was calculated using the radial distribution function. Space et al. pointed out that this distance is an indication that the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules are not directly coordinated to the open $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{2+}$ site, but rather are physisorbed around the site. In this regard, there is no direct interaction between the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule and the open $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ site. In comparison, no peak in this region was found with the models which lack explicit polarization. Here, the nearest interaction distance was found at $3.20\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , indicating that the largest amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules adsorbs in the area of the linker.238
One year later, the same group applied the BSSP model to the corresponding M-MOF-74 analogs with the metals $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ .177 The highest initial $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value of $-14.0\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ was calculated for Ni-MOF-74, followed by Co-MOF-74 with a $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value of $-12.4~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , then for Zn-MOF-74, with the lowest $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value for these MOFs at around $-8.7\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . This GCMC-calculated $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ values are in good agreement with the experimental results (see Table 3). Also, the inverse sigmoidal shape in the isosteric heat of adsorption, which then remains constant at higher loadings, could be reproduced for $\mathrm{Ni\mathrm{-}M O F\mathrm{-}74}~>~\mathrm{Co\mathrm{-}M O F\mathrm{-}74}~>~\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}M O F\mathrm{-}74}.$ . This indicates that the OMS are already fully occupied at lower loadings and there is a large difference in the sorption energetics between the openmetal sites and the secondary adsorption sites, which were observed in the proximity of the neighboring metal-coordinated oxygen atoms. On the contrary, the experimental shape of the heat of adsorption for Zn-MOF-74 decreases only weakly with increasing loading, which is also reflected by the GCMC simulation. This behavior suggests that the energy for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption at the metal atoms is similar to the adsorption energy within the channels. Furthermore, a correlation between the $\mathbf{H}_{2}{-}\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ distance and the $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ values for the corresponding MOFs was found.177
The lowest $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ was discovered in Cu-MOF-74. Space et al. performed electronic structure calculations for Cu-MOF-74 to investigate the reason for the low $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption ability and low isosteric heat of adsorption value in comparison to the M-MOF-74 analogs.241 The results of this study indicate a lower positive partial charge for Cu-MOF-74, compared to the other transition metals, due to the Jahn–Teller effect. This leads to steric strains in the crystal structure and consequently to a weaker interaction of the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site and the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule. GCMC calculations of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and $87\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a nearly constant $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ value of $-6.5~\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ in Cu-MOF-74, which indicates similar energies for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorbed onto the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites and the secondary sites (defined as the oxido groups of the dobdc $^{4-}$ ligands).241 The result that the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites and the secondary adsorption sites are equally energetic in $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption was supported by Dietzel et al.89 This group has shown that the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption properties can also be reproduced using DFT at the PBE level with and without dispersion corrections. In general, calculations at the pure DFT level exhibit an even weaker binding energy for the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site than for the secondary adsorption sites. If dispersion effects were included, a marginally stronger binding energy on the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site than on the secondary adsorption sites was found. In summary, there is a decrease in the difference of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption energy of the open metal site and the secondary adsorption sites according the following order $\mathbf{Ni^{2+}}>\mathbf{Co^{2+}}>\mathbf{Mg^{2+}}>\mathbf{Fe^{2+}}>\mathbf{Zn^{2+}}\sim\mathbf{Mn^{2+}}>\mathbf{Cu^{2+}}.$ 89
For the DFT-simulation of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -bonding in MOFs, the rangeseparated, dispersion-corrected oB97X-D functional has proven to be a suitable and accurate model.242 In addition to thermochemistry and kinetics, it captures covalent and non-covalent interactions. Long et al. predicted differences in electronic structure at the open metal sites of M-MOF-74 $\mathbf{\left[M_{2}(}p$ -dobdc)]) and $\left[\mathbf{M}_{2}(m{\cdot}\mathbf{dobdc})\right]$ with the use of DFT calculations including the oB97X-D functional.88 While both frameworks have approximately the same surface area, their $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption ability is different. For example, it was found that the open $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites show stronger interaction with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules in $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(m{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc})\right]$ compared to Ni-MOF-74. The binding strength in the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ system was examined by energy decomposition analysis (EDA). The advantage of using EDA calculations is that the interpretable physical components are considered individually. Otherwise, when comparing absolute energy differences, a single number is used to describe the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ bond strength. Based on absolutely localized molecular orbitals (ALMOs), the EDA was used to break down the entire binding energies into frozen energy, polarization and charge transfer components.243 In the $p$ -dobdc4 linker MOFs in M-MOF-74, the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binds in a linear end-on geometry while in the $m$ -dobdc $\!^{4-}$ MOFs the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule is oriented bent end-on in the direction of the oxygen atom of the linker.88
DFT calculations with the PBE functional were performed for MOF-505.244 Here, several localizations and orientations of the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule were investigated around the paddle-wheel cluster. The strongest adsorption energy of $-13.44~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ was calculated when the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule was located perpendicularly in the direction of the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site.
## Computational adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ on OMS
Computational investigations applying DFT and GCMC methods predicted several adsorption sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in OMS-MOFs that result in different adsorption enthalpies. To study the interaction energy for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in relation to the different OMS, various computational approaches were used. At low coverage, the CO2 molecules preferentially adsorb on the OMS.155,232,234,245,246 Therefore, we will limit our discussion to the results achieved for the low-pressure regime.
For M-MOF-74, DFT-D studies revealed that the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is end-on coordinated on the OMS and possesses an angular $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}{=}\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}$ orientation.234,249 An orientation towards the linker was assigned to a secondary vdW interaction between the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule and the adjacent oxygen atom of the carboxylate group from the linker.234,245 Here, Wang et al. confirmed that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption onto OMS has contributions from the Lewis acid and base interaction between $\mathbf{O}_{\mathbf{CO}_{2}}$ and the metal ion as well as from the interaction between $\mathbf{C}_{\mathbf{CO}_{2}}$ and the carboxylate oxygen in the square plane of the metal ion.234 Based on various transition metals, Snurr et al. found an explanation for the relative $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding strengths in the effective charge of the metal atom.222 The authors confirmed that a strong positive potential on the metal cation and a strong negative potential around the coordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms cause the angular geometry of the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{O}{=}\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}$ complex. Thus, due to the quadrupole moment of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , there is a lateral interaction between the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule and the oxygen atom from the carboxylate group of the linker.222
DFT calculations including Grimme-dispersion (e.g. PBE-D2, PBE-D3, B3LYP-D, B3LYP- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotD^{\star}}$ , oB97X-D) were employed, with the intention of reproducing experimental heat of adsorption for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in OMS-MOFs.171,220,223,249 Compared to the standard PBE functional, the Grimme D2 and D3 dispersion corrected PBE functional gives more accurate binding energies.171,250 However, Siepmann et al. stated that PBE-D3 underestimates the magnitude of the experimental heats of adsorption for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in M-MOF-74 with a mean unsigned percentage error of $8.4\%$ .171 Smit et al. gave a percent deviation of $\sim6\%$ for the PBE-D3 functional and a slightly smaller deviation of $4.4\%$ for PBE-D2.223
Bordiga et al.249 achieved a ${\Delta H}^{0}$ value of $-37.9\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Mg-MOF-74 using the B3LYP- $\mathbf{\cdotD^{\star}}$ functional, which is somewhat underestimated compared to the experimental data ( $\left(-39\right.$ to $-47\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ .52,69–71,178,222 Likewise, an improved value of $-44.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for Mg-MOF-74 was obtained with the oB97X-D method.220 Both, the oB97X-D and the B3LYP-D functional, however, deliver underestimated $\Delta H$ values for MIL-101(Cr)
$\left(-29.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right.$ and $-30.8\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ compared to the experimental enthalpy at zero coverage of about 44 kJ mol1).220,251
One of the latest computational studies by Yang et al. used DFT-D2 methodology to investigate $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties of several MOFs built from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel units.252 Since the investigated MOFs consist of the same paddle-wheel cluster but differ in the carboxylate ligands, the calculations were performed on a selected ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel cluster model derived from HKUST-1. The development of a combination of Coulomb potentials and vdW interactions (with Morse potential parameters derived from quantum calculations) was required to describe the interaction in the considered system. The DFT-D2 computed interaction energy for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ of $-21.2~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ is underestimated compared to the experimental $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value of 29.0 kJ mol1 (see Table 3).232,252
In addition to the Grimme approach, further adjustments of the standard exchange–correlation functionals were pursued by Lundqvist et $a l.^{253}$ The authors include a nonlocal term to capture long-range interactions such as vdW-forces (vdW-DF functional). A second generation of this approach has been proposed, employing both, a semi-local exchange functional (PW86)254 and a large-N asymptote gradient correction (vdW-DF2), where N refers to the number of electrons.255 DF functionals such as optB88-vdW and optB86b-vdW were used to calculate the binding energies for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in M-MOF-74.223 However, Smit et al. found that these functionals overestimate the experimental values by B8 kJ mol1.223
Sayo´s et al. showed for Mg-MOF-74, that the calculated vdWDF2 zero-coverage adsorption energy at $298\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at the open $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ sites $(-47.9\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ is in good agreement with the experimental result $\left(-45\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ .178,246
Brown et al. indicated that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption ability is determined by the bond strength of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule on the OMS.70 The authors obtained the binding energies using the dispersion-correlation functional vdW- $\scriptstyle\mathbf{DF}2+U.$ The inclusion of the Hubbard-like parameter $U$ that relates single particle potentials to the magnetic and orbitals parameters was recommended to localize the d-states of the transition metals.
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding enthalpies $\Delta H$ (at loadings of $0.167\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}^{2+\ast},$ , which were obtained by including quantum zero-point energies (ZPEs) and finite-temperature thermal energies (TEs) at the level of a harmonic approximation, suggested the trend $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ $(-40.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})>\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}(-37.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})>\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}(\$ (34.1 kJ mol1) $>\mathrm{\bf~Mn}^{2+}$ $\left(-33.9\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)\ >\ \mathrm{\bf{Co}}^{2+}$ $(-33.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})\ >\ \mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ $(-30.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})>\mathrm{\bf{Cu}}^{2+}$ $(-27.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}})$ . Here, the calculated values differed slightly from the experimental data (giving the trend $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}\ \left(-43.5\ \mathrm{kJ}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)\ >\ \mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ $(-38.6\ \mathrm{kJ}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1})\ >\ \mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ $\left(-33.6\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)>\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\left(-33.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)>\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\left(-31.7\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ $>\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\left(-26.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)>\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\left(-22.1\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right))$ and showed a deviating isosteric heat of adsorption for $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ .70
In general, within the M-MOF-74-series, the largest affinity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ from computational studies was found for $\mathbf{Mg-MOF-74}$ , which agrees to the experimental data (see also Section 4, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption).
The higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake ability for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ in comparison to transition metals in the M-MOF-74 series could also be detected with the use of non-polarizable force fields derived from DFT calculations including periodic boundary conditions.256 The calculated probability density plots for Mg-MOF-74 showed a lower mobility of the adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules as well as less obvious secondary adsorption sites (adjacent to the primary site) than in both Co- and Zn-MOF-74 (Fig. 26).
This lower mobility was attributed to the fact that Mg-MOF-74 has a large energetic difference between open metal and secondary adsorption sites. Therefore, all OMS are occupied first. Comparatively, in Co-MOF-74 and Zn-MOF-74 the secondary adsorption sites are more occupied, as here the computed binding energies of OMS and secondary adsorption sites are almost similar.256 In further work, Smit et al. stated that in addition to the electrostatic interaction, the binding strength between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the OMS is influenced by the hybridization between the metal ${\bf d}_{z^{2}}$ orbital and a lone pair on $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . For $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ through ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , the ${\bf d}_{z^{2}}$ orbital is singly occupied, while in ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ this orbital is doubly occupied, and the binding strength decreases.223
The lowest binding enthalpy within the M-MOF-74 series was found for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and was related to the fact that the elongation along the axial ligand oxygen atom impedes the approach of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ towards the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site.
Brown et al. showed that the $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\cdot\cdot{\mathbf{O}}({\mathbf{CO}_{2}})$ distance correlates with the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-CO}_{2}$ binding strength, i.e. longer distances decrease the binding energy, albeit their vdW-DF2 calculated distances were overestimated compared to experimental data.70
Smit et al. furthermore found that for OMS-MOFs the vdWDF2 functional overestimated the bond distances, resulting in a slightly higher binding energy $\left(\Delta E=+1.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right.$ ) compared to the experimental value. Therefore, the rev-vdW-DF2 has been applied to improve the geometries.223 By using this functional the binding energy was overestimated by only $0.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ .
Queen et al. confirmed that the inclusion of vdW dispersion correlations in DFT calculations quantitatively support the structural features associated with the OMS as binding sites for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .156 The authors performed periodic DFT calculations using the dispersion corrected rev-vdW-DF2 $+U$ functional to study the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding interaction in the M-btt $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Cr}^{2+}\right.$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ) series.
In this M-btt series, the primary adsorption sites were found at the OMS and secondary adsorption sites were above the $\mathrm{[{\bfM}_{4}C l]^{7-}}$ cluster, arising through the interaction of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule with the tetrazolate rings of the linker (Fig. 27).
An insight into the adsorptive behavior of the isostructural M-btt MOFs was provided by comparison of the DFT derived $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ binding energies for sites I (open metal site) and II (adsorption site above the $\mathrm{[M_{4}C l]^{7-}}$ cluster).156 While the bond strength at site I depends mainly on the metal identity, the bond strength at site II is independent of the metal and is almost identical for the investigated species. The highest theoretical binding enthalpy on the primary site I was calculated for Fe-btt (as $-51.7\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}},$ , followed by Cr-btt $(-36.6\ \mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ and then Cu-btt $(-29.4~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}})$ ). These values are in very good agreement with the experimental isosteric heats of adsorption determined at zero coverage (see Table 3). The higher binding energy for Fe-btt was related to the shorter calculated $\mathrm{Fe-O(CO_{2})}$ distance of $2.301\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ in comparison to the Jahn–Teller-elongated distances for $\mathbf{Cu(\mathrm{II}){\cdot\cdot\cdot O(C O_{2})}}$ (2.567 Å) and $\mathbf{Cr(\mathrm{II})\cdot\cdot\cdot O(C O_{2})}$ (2.625 Å). A shorter binding distance is normally correlated with a higher binding energy, hence the shorter $\mathrm{Cu\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ distance with lower binding energy is somewhat counter-intuitive. The smaller $\mathrm{Cu\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ than $\mathrm{Cr}\cdot\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}$ distance can be explained by the shorter ionic radii of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}.$ .156 The lower $\mathrm{Cu\cdot\cdot\cdotO}$ binding may result from the stronger electronic repulsion of the doubly-occupied ${\bf d}_{z^{2}}$ orbital in $\mathsf{d}^{9}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ relative to the singlyoccupied ${\bf d}_{z^{2}}$ orbital in $\mathrm{d^{4}{\cdot}C r^{2+}(h i g h{\cdot}s p i n)}$ .
Snurr et al. showed that calculations at the MP2-level give improved values for the binding energies compared to pure DFT methods.222 For the M-MOF-74 series a more refined description of the binding energies for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed at OMS was obtained with the use of relaxed structures obtained from B3LYP in combination with single point energies calculated at the MP2 level (Table S1 in $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ).222
For HKUST-1, the most favorable orientation of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is linear and tilted away from the Cu–Cu axis of the paddle wheel SBU.232 Here, Nachtigall et al. suggested that the interaction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ is modulated by the additional electrostatic interaction with the hydrogen of the trimesic acid and the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ cation of the adjacent paddle-wheel SBU, as well as by the dispersion interaction with organic linkers (Fig. 28).
Using the DFT/CC method for HKUST-1, an interaction energy of $-28.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ was calculated at low coverage with one $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule per ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site, which is consistent with the findings by Rubeˇs et al. using the same method.221,232 Nachtigall et al. added that at a coverage of three $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules per $12{\mathrm{~Cu}}^{2+}$ centers ( $3:12$ coverage), the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules are not statistically distributed over the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites but are preferably adsorbed at the adjacent three ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites within the triangularshaped small cage forming one small opening (Fig. 28).232 Here, an increased interaction energy of $-35.9\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for the third $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule was calculated, because of an additional stabilization due to the T-shaped interaction of adjacent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules (as was also found in M-MOF-74, cf. Fig. 41b).
## Computational adsorption of CO on OMS
The influence of open metal sites on the CO adsorption was investigated with periodic DFT and cluster models including various levels of correlation.221,257–260
Georgiev et al. applied cluster and periodic DFT calculations at the PBE level to study the CO adsorption at open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in Cu-MOF-74 (Fig. 29).73 Both models revealed a weak CO binding at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, which was mainly attributed to dispersive interactions. The calculated binding energies of $-23.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ with the periodic model and $-17.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ with the cluster model were in the range of the adsorption enthalpy of $-20\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ obtained via variable-temperature IR (VTIR) experiments.73
Periodic DFT-D calculations at the B3LYP- $\mathbf{\cdotD^{\star}}$ level of theory were applied to calculate the heats of adsorption of CO on $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}{\cdot}74\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .245,249 The simulations at the $\mathrm{\bfB}3\mathrm{\bfLYP}+\mathrm{\bfD}^{\star}$ level predicted heats of adsorption $\Delta H\left(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\right.$ , $-41.9\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $-30\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ; $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $-24.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ) which underestimate the experimental data $\mathrm{(Ni^{2+},-59.7k J m o l^{-1}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $-38.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}},$ ).178,245,249 Sauer et al. showed that the hybrid $\mathbf{MP2:B3LYP+D^{\star}}$ method gives improved results, although the obtained values still deviate from the experiment $(\mathbf{Ni}^{2+};$ $-50.3\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ; $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $-41.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ; $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , $-39.8\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ).
## Computational adsorption of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ on OMS
Wang et al. predicted that the strong $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ interaction with open OMS in the M-MOF-74 series is caused by the $\upsigma$ -donor/ $\pi$ -acceptor bonding between of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and suitable transition metal atoms, as established in organometallic chemistry.234
Fr¨oba et al. pointed out the need for inclusion of a parameter adjustment based on ab initio calculations to describe the interaction of guest molecules with the OMS.209 To account for electrostatic interactions between the sorbate and the OMS, point charges for the MOF were derived by fitting them to the DFT-PBE calculated electrostatic potential. This method revealed for HKUST-1 an isosteric heat of adsorption of $-30\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at low coverage, which deviates slightly from the experimental $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value ( $-30.4$ to $-39.0\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ).209,261,262
Li et al. performed GCMC simulations and DFT calculations to study the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in a series of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel MOFs, such as MOF-505, Nju-Bai12, PCN-46, ZJU-40 and NOTT-X $\mathbf{\widetildeX}=101$ , 102, 103, 106, 108).263 It was found that OMS, organic ligands and the functionalization of the ligands make an important contribution in promoting $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage capacity and the separation of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ below 2 bar. Quantummechanical calculations were used to capture the interactions of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ with the OMS. The corresponding terms were produced by fitting the DFT energy surface. Optimization of the ground state geometries was performed employing GGA exchange implemented with PBE. In all $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel MOFs, three adsorption sites for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ were found (Fig. 31, left). These sites are referred to the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site (site I), the basket site, where three paddle-wheel clusters and three ligands form a basket (site II) and the sites which are mostly near the aromatic rings of the linker (site III). The basket site II was found to be the most preferred adsorption site for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . For this basket-pocket site higher binding energies (34.0 to $-41.1\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ than for site I $_{-33.7}$ to $-29.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) and for site III $\left(-14.3\mathrm{to}-33.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ were calculated using DFT (Fig. 31, right).263
## Computational adsorption of $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ on OMS
Computational studies for the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption behavior in the paddle-wheel MOFs $\left[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathbf{btc})_{2}\right](\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Cu}^{2+},\mathbf{Cr}^{2+})$ and PCN-14 indicate that the OMS are not the preferred adsorption sites for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .212,264,265
Cavalcante et al. classified the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites in PCN-14 as weak methane adsorption sites.265 GCMC simulated adsorption isotherms performed for HKUST-1 and PCN-14 at $150\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed an occupation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites (1.67 and 0.05 bar). At $290~\mathrm{K}$ over a pressure range of $0.01{-}35\$ bar no adsorption on these sites could be determined theoretically. Likewise, GCMC calculations show no energy barriers between weak and strong adsorption sites in PCN-14, allowing open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites to direct the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules to the neighboring adsorption sites. After all, it was reasoned that multiple adsorption sites and efficient linking between them can explain the high methane uptake in PCN-14.265
Brown et al. stated that, consequently, the strength of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binding at the OMS in HKUST-1 and $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ is caused by intermolecular interactions with adjacent $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules adsorbed at the windows of the octahedral cages (primary sites) instead of an electronic interaction with the metal center.264
The values for the binding enthalpy for the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site in HKUST-1 was calculated to be $-9.4~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ using the DFTvdW- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{D}\mathbf{F}2+U$ method. Comparatively, a significantly higher value for the binding enthalpies at the windows of HKUST-1 $\left(-21.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ and $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right](-21.5\mathrm{~kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ was calculated. The authors noted that the DFT calculations for $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ fail to capture the multireference character of the $\mathbf{Cr-Cr}$ bond in the singlet ground state, and they argued that the detection of bond enthalpies at the open $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ site is therefore unreliable. Furthermore, these simulations revealed no difference in the adsorption behavior of HKUST-1 and $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}];$ , for the interaction with the respective OMS.264 The type of the metal cation affects the adsorption mechanism only very little and the strong binding is due to a combination of structural features that are identical in both $\left[{\bf M}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ materials together with $\mathbf{CH_{4}}\mathbf{\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ vdW interactions. Hence, the uptake values for HKUST-1 and $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ are similar.264
Du¨ren et al. used DFT/CC-PES simulations to derive the favorable adsorption sites in HKUST-1.212 With the intention to eliminate the ambiguity and inaccuracy that results from generic force fields, they applied a potential energy surface (PES) calculated by a hybrid DFT/ab initio method (DFT/CC) in the GCMC simulations. Here, the DFT/CC corrections were performed using PBE with the AVQZ basis set and $\mathrm{ccsp(T)/}$ CBS (complete basis set limit) calculations. The authors noted that simulation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ and $87\mathrm{~K~}$ using the UFF-based GCMC method is not suitable to capture the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption on the OMS. Instead, DFT/CC-PES simulations capture the shape of the experimental adsorption isotherm and the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites including the OMS. This study revealed a population in the centers of the small pores at low $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ loading, followed by adsorption on the center of the windows to the small cavities and only at higher loading an occupancy of the OMS was observed.212 According to the DFT/CC-PES, an occupancy of $96\%$ was determined on the OMS, corresponding to 46.25 of the 48 OMS being occupied by $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Also, the simulated distance between the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules and the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site of 3.097 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ agrees well with the experimental value $\left(3.075{\mathring\mathrm{~A}}\right)$ .
Furthermore, the fluid framework potential energy derived with DFT/CC-PES yields an interaction with the OMS, while this effect was not captured by the PES derived from the UFF force field.212
## Separation
Independent of the open metal source, computational studies using DFT-derived non-polarizable force fields for the adsorption of dry $\mathrm{N_{2}/C O_{2}/C H_{4}}$ gas mixtures reproduce for the whole M-MOF-74 series a higher $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , with almost no ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorbed under natural gas purification conditions.256 Smit et al. developed DFT-derived non-polarizable force fields to study the adsorption properties of the gas mixture $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in M-MOF-74 considering the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ sensitivity of these frameworks. The use of this method allowed for a forecast how fast a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake will decrease in the presence of water. It could be detected that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake decreases with increasing $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ bonding energy. Thus, in Mg-MOF-74, with the highest DFT binding energy for $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(-66.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ uptake is greater than for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , even when the mole fraction of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ in the gas mixture is only $0.1\%$ at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar. In contrast, in Zn-MOF-74, with the weakest DFT-calculated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ binding energy $\left(-46.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ , the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ uptake surpasses the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake only when the mole fraction of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ is above $2\%$ (at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ and 0.15 bar). Thus, Zn-MOF-74 can tolerate the presence of 20-times more $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ than $\mathbf{Mg-MOF-74}$ . The latter will be useless for carbon capture as most flue gases contain significantly more than $2\%$ $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ . The values for the other M-MOF-74 analogues lie in a range between $0.1\%$ and $2\%$ . Consequently, none of the M-MOF-74 frameworks will be applicable for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption from moist gas streams.256
## 4. Open metal site applications
‘‘Application’’ can mean different things. In basic research it could be already considered an ‘‘application’’ that OMS allow for the defined post-synthetic modification of a different linker at the metal site. Further, the formation of an OMS is also the prerequisite for the ‘‘binding’’ of weakly coordinating adsorbates such as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ etc. As outlined in the computational Section 3, these gas molecules bind most strongly to the OMS which forms the basis of ‘‘applying’’ OMS-MOFs for high gas uptake and separation (see below). At the same time, the structure elucidation of the binding mode of these adsorbed gas molecules in OMS-MOFs is also ‘‘applied’’ in structural studies.
In addition, we note that the following ‘‘applications’’ of OMS-MOFs (or MOFs at large) in gas sorption, catalysis and sensing are ‘‘potential applications’’, which are suggested from lab experiments and which have not yet been commercialized.
Noteworthy, this section only collects ‘‘applications’’ in connection with verified OMS formation and does not refer to work which only claims OMS effects.
## Post functionalization of OMS in MOFs
By definition, post-functionalization refers to a chemical modification of the MOF after its synthesis. We emphasize again that the structure of the MOF should be preserved upon functionalization, which needs to be verified e.g. by powder X-ray diffraction, FT-IR spectroscopy and gas sorption experiments after the reaction.12
The general and basic strategy found in the literature for post-functionalization of OMS is a ligand coordination to the OMS. This ligand coordination is also named ‘‘grafting’’98,269,270 in the literature. Also the term ‘‘amination’’ is used,271,272 when an amine ligand is coordinated to the OMS. The term ‘‘extended hook’’273 is found for bifunctional ligands, like ethylenediamine, where one end is anchored to the open metal sites on the framework and the other end is available as an additional functionality in the pore, for example to capture metal ions or clusters at the centers of the channels.12,98,113,143,146,148,182,269–286
Using post-functionalization via grafting a ligand to the open metal sites in MOFs can improve their performance in various applications, for example increase the gas uptake or catalytic activity. It should be evident that the quantification of the amount of grafted ligands to OMS is essential and can be carried out, for example, by MOF digestion and NMR analysis.
We note that the post-functionalization by ligand-coordination or -grafting to metal sites does not necessarily need to proceed via a proven or prepared OMS intermediate. A simple ligand exchange where the solvent–ligand at the metal atom in the MOF framework is replaced by the new (functional) ligand will also achieve this type of post-functionalization. If this ligand exchange proceeds through a more dissociative mechanism, that is, by the existing ligand dissociating first, then OMS will be a shortlived intermediate. However, ligand replacement in coordination chemistry can also occur via an associate mechanism through expansion of the coordination number.
In this section we will only discuss post-functionalization by ligand-coordination or -grafting which occurred via a verified OMS intermediate.
## Post-functionalization of open ${\bf{C r}}^{3+}$ sites
Kim et al.143 reported on the post-functionalized MIL-101(Cr) compounds, CMIL-1 and CMIL-2, where the chiral pyridyl modified organocatalytic L-proline ligands L1 $[(S)-N\cdot$ (pyridin3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] and L2 [(S)-N-(pyridin-4-yl)- pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] were coordinated to the open $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites. The CMIL compounds showed catalytic activities in asymmetric aldol reactions and by comparison with the chiral ligands alone a much higher enantioselectivity. For the post-functionalization, the activated MOF and the new ligand were mixed together in anhydrous chloroform and refluxed under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The structure of MIL-101(Cr) did not change after grafting with the chiral ligands based on PXRD. The success of the grafting was based on FT-IR spectroscopy which showed characteristic bands of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ at 1558 and $1695~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , and $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ stretching bands at 3189 and $3220~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and on elemental analysis which suggested that $\sim1.8$ chiral ligands per formula unit were incorporated inside the pores of CMILs. The BET surface area decreased from 3850 (for MIL-101(Cr)) to 1420 (for CMIL-1) and to $1375~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (for CMIL-2), due to the incorporation of the organic molecules inside the pores.143 Along the same lines, Ren et al.113 grafted the chiral pyridyl modified L-proline derivative [(S)-1-formyl-N-(pyridin-3-yl)pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] to the open $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites of the MIL-101(Cr) framework. They used the same methods as Kim et al.,143 to check the successful introduction of the ligand. The elemental analysis results indicated that 1.45 chiral ligands per formula unit are incorporated inside the pores of CMIL-101 and the BET surface area decreased from 2963 to $1258\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , due to of the immobilization of organic molecules inside the pores (Fig. 32).113
Furthermore, Jhung et al.269 reported on MIL-101(Cr) with or without modification for the adsorptive removal of the artificial sweeteners saccharin, acesulfame and cyclamate. For the postmodification of MIL-101(Cr), the activated MIL and melamine or urea was mixed together in toluene solvent and refluxed for 12 hours. Under these conditions and low basicity, the amino groups of melamine or urea were grafted onto the open $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites. FT-IR spectroscopy showed characteristic bands of N–H bending, C–N stretching, and $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ wagging at 1608 and 1265/ $1211~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $707~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the BET surface area decreased from 3030 (for MIL-101(Cr)) to 1970 (for urea MIL-101) and to $1350~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (for melamine MIL-101), indicating the successful introduction of urea and melamine into MIL-101(Cr). It must be noted that the study did not quantify the amount of grafting.269 Moreover, Janiak et al.138 grafted several glycols ethylene glycols and ethylenediamine onto MIL-100(Cr) and quantified the amount of grafting, by using solution NMR spectroscopy. The $\mathbf{\Omega}^{1}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{NMR}$ spectra showed signals of the deprotonated and deuterium exchanged ligand $\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{3}(\mathrm{COO}^{-})_{3}$ , $\displaystyle\bigl(\mathsf{b t c}^{3-}\bigr)$ and the grafted reagents (Fig. 33) and the integration gave ranges of grafted glycols and amine in MIL-100(Cr) from 1.28 to 1.88 molar equivalents for each $\left[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathsf{\mu_{3}{-}O})(\mathrm{F},\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\right]$ formula unit.138
## Post-functionalization of open ${\bf{C u}}^{2+}$ sites
Liu et al.98 reported on the grafting with pyridyl-salicylimine (Py-SI) to open metal sites in Cu-bdc MOF MOF-2 by stirring the ligand and activated Cu-bdc in dry toluene. Afterwards, a solution of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ , toluene and DMF was added to Cu-bdc/Py-SI to obtain a Pd@Cu-bdc/Py-SI catalyst for Suzuki coupling (Fig. 34). They based the successful grafting on SEM-EDX quantification of Cl, Pd, and N. To enhance the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture in $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}[(\mathrm{{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{{btt}_{r i})_{8}]}}}$ Long et al.278 grafted $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) to the Cu sites to yield $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}[(\mathrm{{Cu}}_{4}\mathrm{{Cl})}_{3}.$ $\mathrm{(btt_{ri})_{8}(m m e n)_{12}]}$ .287,288 However, half of the Cu-OMS were not grafted at all, as the grafting was successful only at the large pores. The grafted material did not collapse and retained the original structure according to PXRD. From ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption measurement the BET surface area decreased from $1770\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $870\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ .278 Rosseinsky et al.274 described the binding of secondary amines to the coordination sites on the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle-wheel MOF HKUST-1. The modified HKUST-1 was then exposed to NO to form a coordinated $N_{\mathbf{\lambda}}$ -diazenium diolates (NONOates).274
## Gas sorption
Gas adsorption, including harmful gases is an issue of constant interest in MOF chemistry.79,289 The amount and affinity of the adsorbed gas depends on adsorbent (MOF) binding sites, surface area, porosity, temperature, pressure and the polarity of the adsorbate (gas).290 MOFs have been widely studied with respect to the selective adsorption and separation of gases such as $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , CO, NO, $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ .291,292 Open metal sites and large surface area in MOFs are two important parameters for adsorption capacity and efficiency. Stable MOFs with accessible open metal sites have a higher gas affinity and binding energy compared to the same MOF without OMS.293 The somewhat higher porosity of OMS-MOFs also leads to a higher gas uptake. Gas adsorption to OMS is typically reflected in a steeper rise of the adsorption isotherm and a higher (negative) heat of adsorption $(Q_{s\mathrm{t}}^{0})$ at low loading compared to the same non-OMS MOF.220 Such a steep rise and high $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ reflects the affinity, that is preferential binding of the gas molecules to the free metal site. We note that the comparison of adsorption capacity or gas uptake values at $p/p_{0}=1$ or 1 bar absolute pressure is less meaningful since these values are largely determined by pore volume.67,251
In connection with gas sorption studies on OMSMOFs, experimental high-resolution70,155/powder neutron diffraction70,159,228,294–296 studies, synchrotron X-ray diffraction analysis $^{155,178,297,298}$ and near sites edge X-ray adsorption fine structure (NEXAFS)299 spectroscopy were performed to obtain structural information of MOFs with OMS and to derive insight into the role of the OMS on the MOF–adsorbate interactions.300
One must admit that single crystal X-ray studies which require sizeable and good quality single crystals are seldom available for OMS-MOFs with adsorbates (see below for examples). We note that often MOF structures, e.g., for HKUST-1, are reported with no solvent–ligand at the metal atom. Usually, the electron density which is associated with this solvent ligand then has not been refined or has been removed by the Squeeze option in the refinement procedure.301–303 The knowledge about the interaction and dynamics between the OMS and adsorbate molecules is crucial for the design of frameworks with efficient gas capture possibilities. However, it is evident that in order to study OMS– adsorbate interactions by experimental structure methods, a fully activated sample without remaining coordinated solvent ligands on the metal atom is highly advantageous to induce and have only the adsorbate bound to the metal atoms.155
## $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption
In the early but superseded realm of hydrogen storage in MOFs metal–organic frameworks with open metal sites were seen as an important class for hydrogen sorption. The X-ray and neutron diffraction studies showed that OMS are the understandable primary location for adsorbed $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules. For OMS only the low pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption is relevant. Due to the good hydrogen affinity, they can slightly increase the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity and improve the isosteric heat of adsorption at low coverage $(Q_{s\mathrm{t}}^{0})$ . However, due to the still relatively weak interactions with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , MOFs were not able not meet the gravimetric and volumetric storage targets.86–88,91,304–306
The most investigated series of metal–organic frameworks is the M-MOF-74 family consisting of, e.g., $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(p{\cdot}\mathbf{dobdc})],$ [M2(m-dobdc)], $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})];$ , and $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})],$ , which possess a high concentration of OMS, which can interact more strongly with the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules. The isosteric heats of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption in $\left[\mathbf{M}_{2}(p{\cdot}\mathbf{dobdc})\right]$ $\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+})$ indicated initial stronger binding sites and lower enthalpies of adsorption upon increasing adsorption. From the quantitative assessment of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake a change in adsorption isotherm shape occurs upon reaching approx. one equivalent of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ per metal site. The isosteric heat dropped from about $8–13\mathrm{~kJ\mol^{-1}}$ (depending on the metal, see Fig. 35) to a lower value of about $5{-}6\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ after saturation of the stronger binding sites at a loading of $\sim0.8$ molecule $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ per metal. $^{88,89,91,307-309}$ It can be noted that conversely the loading of ${\sim}0.8\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}$ per mol metal may represent a quantification of the amount of formed OMS if the alternative of missing-metal defects or pore blocking can be excluded.
Based on neutron diffraction the presence of primary strong $\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{D}_{2}$ binding sites and several additional weaker binding sites was elucidated for M-MOF-74. Neutron diffraction studies with M-MOF-74 revealed metal-to- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf D}_{2}$ distances for the primary binding site of $2.20(1)^{-3.03}$ Å with ${\bf N i}^{2+}<{\bf C o}^{2+}<{\bf M g}^{2+}<$ $\mathrm{Fe^{2+}<Z n^{2+}<M n^{2+}<C u^{2+}}$ (Fig. 36). The metal-to- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf D}_{2}$ bond distances correlate with the adsorption enthalpies, with ordering as $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}^{2+}<\mathbf{M}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}\sim\mathbf{Z}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}<\mathbf{F}\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{2+}<\mathbf{M}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{2+}<\mathbf{C}\ensuremath{\mathbf{o}}^{2+}<\mathbf{N}\ensuremath{\mathrm{i}}^{2+}.$ 07,308
In isoreticular MOFs the $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ ordering is $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}<\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2+}<\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{2+}$ $<\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ which is inversely proportional to the ionic radius of the metal ions which steadily drops from (high spin) $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ (Fig. 37).88,310 As noted above for Fig. 35, the inflection points in Fig. 37 from $\sim0.75~\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}$ in $\left[{\bf C o}_{2}(m{\cdot}{\bf d o b d c})\right]$ to $\sim0.85\ \mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{M}$ in $[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(m{\cdot}\mathrm{dobdc})]_{:}$ , may be interpretation as the fraction of OMS generated and available for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binding. The inflection points are similar to the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -isosteric heat values for the M-MOF-74 series shown in Fig. 35.88
In case of $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})]$ the isosteric heat curve had the inflection point only for $\left[\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\right]$ and $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})]$ at an $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ loading of one molecule per mol metal while for $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{2+}$ $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ the inflection point is at a lower loading than 1 eq. of ${\bf H}_{2}/{\bf M}$ , as defects or intergrowth of crystallites may block some of the channels or the OMS are not quantitatively formed (Fig. 38). This can explain why Ni-MOF-74 displayed a higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity at pressures up to approx. 300 mbar (at 77 K) than $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})]$ . While at the same time, $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})]$ showed a higher gravimetric density of OMS (6.42 vs. $5.16\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega}$ and a higher low-coverage isosteric heat. Noteworthy also $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ showed the lowest value, due to its $3\mathrm{d}^{10}$ electron configuration.309
The charge density at the metal sites and thus the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binding enthalpies could be enhanced by replacing the linker $p$ -dobdc $\!^{4-}$ with $m$ -dobdc $^{4-}$ , thereby going from the M-MOF-74 to [M2(m-dobdc)] $\left(\mathbf{M}\right)=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Co}^{2+},\ \mathrm{Ni}^{2+})$ (Table 3). The stronger interaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and the metal centers in the latter is evidenced by shorter $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ contacts based on the results from powder neutron diffraction, inelastic spectroscopy and IR spectroscopy experiments.88
The MOFs, $\left[{\bf M}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})\right]$ $\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{olz}^{2-}={}$ olsalazinate), exhibit the same SBU and ligand coordinating functionality as $[{\bf M}_{2}(p$ -dobdc)].310 The isosteric heats od adsorption values for $\big[\mathbf{M}_{2}\big(\mathrm{olz}\big)\big]$ are very similar to $\left[\mathbf{M}_{2}(m{\cdot}\mathbf{dobdc})\right]$ and $\left[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\right]$ (Fig. 39, left).310 The inflection points in the isosteric heat plot of Fig. 39, left indicate the amount of available OMS. For $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})\right]$ the inflection point at $\mathbf{\sigma}^{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal’ of about 1 shows near complete preceding activation and Co–OMS availability. The MOF $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})\right]$ and $\big[\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})\big]$ still have the inflection point at about $85\%$ and $70\%$ $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal’, respectively. A significant lower inflection point, i.e. OMS availability of about $0.6\ \cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal’ is seen for $[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})],$ which was explained with the milder activation conditions employed for this compound.
In situ IR spectroscopy was used to determine the nature of the $\left[\mathbf{Mg}_{2}/\mathbf{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{olz})\right]$ interaction with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . To follow the binding of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at the OMS the characteristic red-shift of the $\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching frequency can be used. IR spectra (Fig. 39, right) showed a doublet (4097 and $4091~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}},$ ) at a loading of one $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ per metal. The authors described that these are the pure vibrational modes of ortho- ${\bf\cdot H}_{2}$ and para- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bound to the OMS for 1 eq. ${\bf H}_{2}/{\bf M}$ .310
The curves of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ isosteric heats of adsorption versus the amount of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal in Fig. 35, 37, 38 and 39, left allow to estimate the amount of available OMS. Thereby it can be assumed that ‘/metal’ means total molar metal amount based on the sample mass and idealized formula unit. The isosteric heat of adsorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ data can be obtained from low-pressure $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ isotherms at two different temperatures, e.g. 77 and $87\mathrm{~K~}$ using the Clausius–Clapeyron relation.
The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ amount at which the isosteric heat decreases (the inflection point), can be taken as an indicator for the amount and the accessibility of the OMS. If the inflection point is at $\mathbf{\sigma}^{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorbed/metal’ $\approx1$ then the framework was probably fully activated and all OMS were accessible to $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . If the inflection point in the isosteric heat curve is at $\cdot_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ adsorbed/metal’ $^{<1}$ , then part of the OMS is not accessible to $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . This could be due incomplete activation that is incomplete solvent removal or missing-metal (or other) defects or pore-blocking in the channels which renders the metal sites inaccessible to gas molecules.
M-btt $\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{Cr}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ was one of the first OMS MOF series which was studied for hydrogen storage (Table 3).157–160,311 As noted above Fe-btt exhibits a higher isosteric heat of adsorption compared to Mn-btt, due to the smaller radius for $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (higher charge-to-radius ratio), giving a more polarizing binding site at the Fe ion.309,311 Powder neutron diffraction of ${\bf D}_{2}$ -loaded Cr-btt159 suggested only moderately strong $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}{\bf-D}_{2}$ interactions from the rather long $\mathrm{Cr-D}_{2}$ distance. However, the isosteric heat at low coverage was higher for Cr-btt compared to $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]^{135}$ (Table 3). $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}],$ with the same paddle-wheel SBU as HKUST-1. It was suggested that the $\mathbf{Cr\equiv}\mathbf{Cr}$ quadropole bond decreases the Lewis acidity of the Cr2+ centers.157–160,311
Paddle-wheel MOFs, like HKUST-1, NOTT-X, PCN-10,-11 with OMS feature in general comparatively low $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ values of less than $-7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (Table 3). It should be noted that tor ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in HKUST-1 and related MOFs its Jahn–Teller effect provides no strong driving force for coordination of gas molecules at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site.135
Accordingly, the series of NOTT- $X$ $(X=100–107)$ MOFs with the paddle-wheel SBU showed isosteric heats of adsorption at low coverage in the range of 5.20 to $6.70\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (Table 3) and the enthalpy of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binding is influenced by pore size and the presence of additional aromatic rings not the OMS.312
Still, the paddle-wheel OMS-MOFs have a higher hydrogen affinity at low pressures in comparison to MOFs without OMS. The isosteric heat of adsorption in HKUST-1 $\left(-4.5\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ , Table 3) is higher than in MOF-5 $\left(-3.8\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ , due to the steep rise in the adsorption isotherm.313
In summary, open metal sites MOFs with $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ , Mn, Fe, Co, Ni are the most promising candidates for hydrogen sorption at low pressures, due to the higher enthalpy of adsorption of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at these metal ions (isosteric heat of adsorption from about $^{-10}$ to $-13.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1},$ ). The isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption to the MOF pore wall is only around 3.8 kJ mol1.313
## $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption
The interaction between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and OMS is generally more than $30~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol}^{-1}$ , giving a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low relative pressure.67,251,314 The binding affinity between the OMS and the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbate is correlated with electrostatic interactions ( $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a quadrupole moment).315 The isostructural frameworks M-MOF-74 $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}\right.$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{\bfNi}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ) have been extensively studied for their high performance in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ storage.52,70,316–319 Since these frameworks can be synthesized with different metals, they reflect both the role of each metal and the degree of Lewis acid interaction between the metal center and the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. In particular, the presence of the stronger $\mathbf{CO_{2}}\mathrm{-OMS}$ binding and a high density of OMS within the framework led to large $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes and makes this MOF group an interesting candidate for CO2 adsorption.52,97 Especially at low pressures (0.1–0.2 bar), which is the pressure region of interest in flue gas separation, these OMS-MOFs show high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptakes.55
Kortrigh et al. applied ‘near sites edge X-ray absorption fine structure’ (NEXAFS) spectroscopy measurements at the Mg K-edge to study the interactions of Mg-MOF-74 with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules at open $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ sites.299 The authors associated the square pyramidal geometry at the open $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ site with the appearance of a pre-edge peak in the Mg K-edge spectrum. A reduction in the intensity of this pre-edge feature, accompanied by a slight blue shift and an increase in intensity of the strongest major edge feature upon adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , is interpreted as a recovery of the octahedral geometry around the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$ cation with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ coordination. This interpretation of the experimental results was supported with DFT calculations.299
Yildirim et al. showed by neutron powder diffraction measurements for the example of Mg-MOF-74, that the OMS in the M-MOF-74 series represent the primary adsorption sites.228 Subsequently, the occupation of other $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites within the framework was later authenticated by neutron diffraction experiments and synchrotron X-ray diffraction measurements and was further supported by in situ $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR measurements.70,178,294,320
As is shown in Fig. 40 the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule assumes a bent endon configuration when binding to metal atoms, as was detected for OMS in M-MOF-74 $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}\right.$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ) via powder neutron diffraction experiments.70
Moon et al. performed single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed single crystals of $\mathbf{Mg-}$ , Co- and NiMOF-74, to obtain insight into the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites at the atomic level.178 Also, two non-independent adsorption sites within the framework were found, which were assigned to the OMS (site A) and a secondary adsorption site between two OMS (site B) (Fig. 41).178
According to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $298~\mathrm{~K~}$ the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity of ${\bf M}\mathrm{g}{\bf-}$ , Co- and Ni-MOF-74 are 0.81, 1.30 and $1.67~\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively, at 0.1 bar.178 The experimental obtained $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ values for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at OMS in the M-MOF-74 series are in the range from $-39.0$ to $47.0\ \mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ to $-22.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ following the order $\mathbf{Mg^{2+}}>\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}>$ $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\sim\mathbf{Fe}^{2+}>\mathbf{Mn}^{2+}>\mathbf{Zn}^{2+}>\mathbf{Cu}^{2+}$ .52,70,71,222,297
Adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at the OMS should also be reflected in the observation of an $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O}}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ ‘bond’ distance. Findings from single-crystal X-ray diffraction revealed short $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O}}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ bond distances, $\left(\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})\right.$ , 2.13(2) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\mathrm{-O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ , 2.27(2) Å; $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}{\mathrm{-O}}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ , $2.08(6)\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ , indicating the strong affinity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules.178 Single-crystal X-ray diffraction data for Mg- and Co-MOF-74 show interactions, of each $\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ atom in site B with the $\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ atom in site A, resulting in a T-shaped arrangement of adjacent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules (Fig. 41a and b). For the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule adsorbed on Ni-MOf-74, a slipped-parallel geometry was indicated (Fig. 41c).178
The values for the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distances determined via high-resolution powder neutron diffraction experiments were $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}{\mathrm{-O(CO_{2})}}$ , 2.27(1) Å; $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\mathrm{-O(CO_{2})}$ , 2.23(9) Å; $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ , $2.29\overset{}{\underset{}{(3)}}\overset{\circ}{\mathbf{A}}$ (taken at a temperature of 10 K) and differ only slightly from the single-crystal X-ray diffraction values.70 The elongated $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-O(CO}_{2})}$ distances found for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}\left(2.51{\left(3\right)}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}\right)$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{C}_{\mathsf{U}}}^{2+}\left(2.86\bigl(3\right)\mathring{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{A}}}\right)$ and $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\left(2.43(4)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ analogues reflect their lower isosteric heats of adsorption. For $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ this has been explained by its larger ionic radius.70 It can be noted that for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ its Jahn–Teller effect together with an overall lower effective ionic charge of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion provide no strong driving force for coordination of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule at the apical ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site in Cu-MOF-74.297
It has been shown that the adsorption capacity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can vary depending on the metal species. Semilogarithmic plots of the adsorption isotherms depict a two-step adsorption, indicating a sequential occupation of the adsorption sites.222,297,320,321 The first step at low loadings was assigned to the adsorption on the OMS (site A in Fig. 41).297 Depending on the metal species, it occurs at increasing pressure in the order $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}<\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}<\mathbf{Co}^{2+}<$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}<\ensuremath{\mathbf{Z}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}\ll\ensuremath{\mathbf{C}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{u}}^{2+}$ . At higher pressures the occupation of the secondary adsorption sites (site B in Fig. 41) occurs with the same metal order.297
A significantly lower $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value of $-22.1\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ and a nearly linear uptake for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ is evidence that there is no decisive preference for adsorbing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at the apical ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ OMS or in the pores (Fig. 42).70
Further, the $\mathbf{Cu-O-C(CO_{2})}$ angle was estimated to be $121^{\circ}$ , while the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}\to\mathbf{O}-\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})$ angles of the M-MOF-74 analogs are in the range of $145\substack{-150}^{\circ}$ .297 The small bond angle for Cu-MOF-74 together with the weaker $\mathbf{Cu-O}$ interaction, indicates a strong influence of van der Waals interactions between $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and the adjacent pore wall of the dobdc $^{4-}$ ligand.
The dependency of the adsorption strength on the temperature in Mg-MOF-74 was investigated by Long et al. $^{26}\mathbf{Mg-MOF-}74$ shows an initial steep $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low pressures which is characteristic for high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ affinity. Huang et al. performed variable-temperature (VT) $^{17}\mathrm{O}$ solid-state NMR spectroscopy (SSNMR) from $150\mathrm{~K~}$ to $403~\mathrm{K},$ to characterize the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of MOFs as a function of the metal– $\mathbf{\cdotCO}_{2}$ bond strength.327 The authors stated that the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is attached in a minimum energy configuration to the OMS, with a temperature-dependent angle $\mathbf{Mg-O-C(CO_{2})}$ of ca. $120{-}130^{\circ}$ (Fig. 44). Variable temperature $^{17}\mathrm{O}$ SSNMR line shapes were interpreted by a wobbling of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ about its axis and a hopping between different adsorption sites (Fig. 44). A reduced mobility of the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules at low temperatures leads to an increased interaction with the OMS and vice versa. With increasing temperature to about $403\mathrm{~K~}$ wobbling increases and remains in the fast motion regime $\left(107~\mathrm{Hz}\right)$ , indicating that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ moves through a larger space. On the other hand, the hopping angle decreases with increasing temperature, which was attributed to be a result of enhanced $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ motion along the length of the channels.
In HKUST-1 two primary adsorption sites were revealed by the Fourier difference maps.228 One adsorption site is associated to the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site and the other is associated to ‘‘the small cage window site, where the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sits in the four triangular-shaped openings into the small octahedral cage.’’ Neutron diffraction measurement on the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in HKUST-1, for two different $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loadings (1.07 and $1.47~\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ Cu), revealed that most $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules prefer to adsorb on the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites at low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ concentration. The preferred adsorption on the OMS was associated with the higher electrostatic interaction between the OMS and the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ quadrupole moment compared to an otherwise near vdW interaction between the organic framework walls and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Only with increased $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loading, adsorption on the center of the small octahedral cage and the corner of the large cuboctahedral cage, was observed.228
Nachtigall et al. calculated the heat of adsorption from the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ of approx. $-29\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at low coverage. A slightly increase of heat of adsorption to approx. $-32~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ was observed with higher loading $\left(>8\mathrm{\mmol\g}^{-1}\right)$ .232
Supported by DFT calculations, this behavior was explained as follows: The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorb onto $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites at low coverage. The adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules are tilted towards adjacent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site, which leads to maximized lateral $\scriptstyle\mathbf{CO}_{2}-\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ interactions. With increasing coverage (up to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}=20:12$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ ) the occupation takes place in the cage windows of small cages. Here, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules in cage window sites are stabilized through lateral interactions with the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site. At even higher coverages, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules adsorb in the center of small cages and in large cages. The increasing adsorption enthalpies which were found in the highcoverage regime, were attributed to lateral interactions between these molecules and those already adsorbed in ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites and cage windows sites.232
For $\mathbf{MIL-100(Cr)}$ , the isosteric heat of adsorption $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ of $-58\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ was calculated from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at 273, 298, and $323\mathrm{~K~}$ at low coverage.328 Usually, the value for the heat of adsorption can be obtained from adsorption isotherms at two different temperatures by applying the Clausius– Clapeyron equation.329 From variable-temperature infrared (VTIR) spectroscopy standard adsorption enthalpies for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be obtained from analysis of IR spectra recorded over a temperature range while simultaneously measuring the equilibrium pressure inside a closed IR cell.140 This study, revealed the following heats of adsorption for MIL-100(Cr) $(-63\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}})$ , MIL-100(V) $\left(-54\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ and MIL-100(Sc) $\left(-48\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}\right)$ .140
In comparison F´erey et al. reported an adsorption enthalpy of $-62~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ for MIL-100(Cr) from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms coupled with microcalorimetric experiments, that is in good agreement with the value delivered form VTIR spectroscopy analysis.251
The asymmetric stretching vibration ( $\dot{\nu}_{3}$ mode) of free $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ $\left(2349\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}\right).$ ) remains essentially invariant upon adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in for MIL-100(Cr) $(2349~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , MIL-100(V) $(2348~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and MIL-100(Sc) $(2352~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ ).140 However, Palomino et al. did not take the vibration of free $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ as a reference but the value of $2341~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ for carbon dioxide confined in the pores of silicalite.330 Relative to this value of $2341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ the above blue-shifted values near $2350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were interpreted as being due to a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption onto open $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaV^{3+}}$ and $\sec^{3+}$ sites.140
Also, Fe´rey et al. associated the strong $\nu_{3}–\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ band at $2351\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for MIL-100(Cr) after $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption with the formation of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -coordinated species on the Lewis acid sites $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{O}=\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ $\cdots\cdot\cdot\mathbf{Cr}^{3+})$ .251 In addition, a weak band was observed at $1271~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is assigned to the $2\nu2$ overtone in Fermi resonance with the $\nu_{1}$ mode near $1370~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and which is infrared-inactive for the free $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule. It was also noted that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on MIL-100(Cr) with the aqua ligands still present on the Lewis acid sites did not exhibit the $2351~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band.251
Microcalorimetric experiments for MIL-100(Fe) were also performed, to investigate the energetics of interactions between iron sites and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ during gravimetric measurements of adsorption.142 Here, the heat of adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ for MIL-100(Fe), lies in the range of $-25\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ and $-30\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , for the sample (activated at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ where only $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites are present and $-35\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for the partially reduced sample (activated at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ where $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ are present (see solvent removal strategies).142
The most recent computational studies through a periodic quantum mechanical hybrid HF/DFT approach of MIL- $\mathbf{\cdot}100(\mathbf{M}^{3+})$ structures with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , revealed that $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ interacts strongly with the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule showing a short $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}{-}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ distance (2.35 Å).331 This was explained by the high electrostatic potential and the role of partly occupied d-orbitals (Fig. 45). In comparison $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ shows a longer $\mathrm{Fe-O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ distance $(2.46\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and a smaller electrostatic potential thus leading to a weaker interaction energy.
Low-pressure adsorption isotherms on M-btt $\left(\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{Cr}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+},\mathbf{Fe}^{2+},\mathbf{Cu}^{2+})$ , at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ exhibit a steep initial rise, which is characteristic for the presence of highly polarizing adsorption sites (Fig. 46).156 The steepest rise in the adoption isotherm was observed for Fe-btt, indicating a stronger interaction with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in comparison to its analogues. The calculated isosteric heats of adsorption at zero coverage were in the range of $-51.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ to $-30.7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , following the order $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}>\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}>\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}>\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ . Queen et al. showed, that the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-CO}_{2}$ adsorption strength is reflected in the increase in the bond distance range from 2.36(3) $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ for Fe-btt to 2.60(3) Å for Cu-btt.156
Also, structural investigations via high-resolution powder neutron diffraction experiments of M-btt reveal the expected preferred adsorption of the guest molecules on the OMS (Fig. 47). However, it was pointed out156 that Mn-btt is not suitable for these in situ diffraction experiments, as $83\%$ of the OMS remained coordinated by methanol solvent molecules after activation (based on a previous X-ray analysis).160
In $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{mcapz})\right]$ a steep uptake of 2.3 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was investigated at lower pressure, corresponding to the adsorption of one molecule $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at each dinuclear $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}$ unit.100
Furthermore, it has been shown, that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddle wheel MOFs Cu-tdpat and Cu-Sp5, with both OMS and Lewis basic sites (LBS) within the linker, display an improved $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. The adsorption enthalpy at zero loading of $-42.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Cu-tdpat and $-43.1\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Cu-Sp5 was calculated from the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ sorption data.332 Feng et al. noted that these higher than expected (see above) adsorption enthalpies for Cu-tdpat and Cu-Sp5 are not only caused by the interaction with the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site, but also influenced by further adsorption sites (LBS and phenyl rings) as was investigated with room-temperature IR absorption measurements.333
Zhu et al. found that in the framework of $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{tzpa})(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})],$ the existence of accessible OMS, $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups, and uncoordinated carboxylate O sites, cause strong affinity towards $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .181
## CO adsorption
MOFs with OMS have been proven to show a high heat of adsorption for CO at zero loading. In the literature the dipole moment of CO is sometimes invoked to explain the CO interaction with the metal at the OMS. We note however, that the dipole of CO is only 0.10 Debye with the negative end at the carbon atom. Instead and as can be expected from wellestablished metal–carbonyl chemistry, CO is an excellent ligand with strong covalent interaction to transition-metal atoms. When complete occupancy of the OMS has been achieved the dispersive forces between CO molecules and the organic pore walls of the network become effective. This leads to a decrease in the adsorption enthalpy. Based on the experimentally determined heat of adsorption for the M-MOF-74 series, it was found that the CO affinity for the OMS follows the order $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}^{2+}>$ $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\gg\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}>\mathbf{Cu}^{2+}$ , indicating strong interaction of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ for CO molecules.73,178
The heat of adsorption values for CO in M-MOF-74 reflect the influence of the metal atom on the CO affinity. Moon et al. found the strongest affinity to CO for $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , reaching a $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value of $-59.7\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , followed by ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ with $-55.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ with a significantly lower value of $-38.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (the lower value was explained due the lack of d-valence electrons in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ see above).178 FT-IR analysis revealed stretching vibrations of CO bound to ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ at $2160~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ at $2170~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ , which compared with free CO at $2143~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ would indicate only a $\upsigma$ -donor bond from CO to the metal with no metal-to-CO backbonding. No CO vibration was observed for Mg-MOF-74.178
It is noted that in comparison to the valence band for free CO at $2143~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ the above higher wavenumbers indicate an only very weak M-to-CO back bonding, due to the relatively high metal $^{2+}$ oxidation state. CO is known to prefer to bind to metal atoms in low oxidation state (0 or even negative) as in neutral metal carbonyls or negative carbonyl metallates.23,24
To obtain insight into the CO adsorption sites at the atomic level, Moon et al. performed Single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments with CO-adsorbed single crystals of Mg-, Co- and Ni-MOF-74. The CO molecules were bound to unsaturated transition metal centers with $\mathrm{Co^{2+}/N i^{2+}{-C O}}$ bond distances of 2.151(2) and $2.147\widehat{\left(3\right)}\widehat{\textbf{A}}$ respectively, as seen before in metal carbonyls. As expected the $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}$   CO distance for the nonclassical main-group metal–carbonyl bond is much longer with $2.420(2)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.$ Additional adsorbed CO molecules (at site B) show only van der Waals interactions to the metal-bound CO molecules at site A (Fig. 48).178
For Cu-MOF-74, an even low adsorption enthalpy of approx. $-20~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ was indicated by variable temperature IR experiments.73 Georgiev et al. observed the formation of carbonyls with the participation of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites, by the appearance of intense IR bands at $2153{-}2149~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . It was found that with low coverage, the carbonyl band dominates at $2153~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which converts into a band at $2150~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with increased coverage. With further coverage increase, the band is additionally and gradually redshifted. We note that this comparatively high wavenumber for the CO vibrations suggests very weak interactions with the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ adsorption site. Further a band at $2136~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was referred to physically adsorbed CO. Also, a small fraction of the copper ions was found in the $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ state, indicated by the weak IR band at $2120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Indeed, this band is the only one with remarkable stability towards outgassing.
The authors assumed, that the relatively constant intensity of this band indicates that the respective $\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ sites are formed on specific structures, most probably at the external MOF surface.73
Kitagawa et al. noted accordingly, that despite the presence of open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites in $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{dmcapz})\right]$ , the CO adsorption at $195\mathrm{K}$ is negligible for this compound.100
Lueking et al. performed in situ FTIR measurements on the paddle-wheel MOFs HKUST-1 and Cu-tdpat. FITR spectroscopy was able to detect interaction between CO and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in HKUST-1.334 Here, in 0.34 bar CO at $150\mathrm{~K~}$ , the CO vibration was observed at $2169~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . It should be evident that the high wavenumber signals again a non-classical metal carbonyl with a weak $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$   CO bond due to the non-existing $\pi$ -back bonding (Fig. 49). Furthermore, CO vibrations at $2120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were found, which were assigned to interactions of CO with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ . After Ar purging, broad shoulders at $2156~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2191~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappeared as excess CO was removed, while modes at $2172\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ remained in the spectra. Also, the blue-shift from $2169~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $2172~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the ${\bf C O-C u}^{2+}$ mode with removal of gaseous CO was attributed to a decrease in the number of CO interacting with the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .
At the same time, the CO spectra of Cu-tdpat revealed no remaining Cu-adsorption after Ar purging for CO removal, despite the similar axial ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ adsorption site as in HKUST-1.
Further investigations via temperature programmed desorption (TPD) measurements, showed a virtually identical profile of CO adsorbed to Cu-tdpat and HKUST-1, suggesting that CO adsorbs more strongly to the open metal site. Also, it was found that the amount of CO adsorbed at the axial $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites of Cu-tdpat exceeds that of HKUST-1 up to temperatures above $150\mathrm{K}$ .334 Gumma et al. examined the effect of the CO polarity and the electrostatic CO–OMS interactions on CO-loading in MIL-101(Cr) and HKUST-1, from CO adsorption measurements.335 Lowpressure CO adsorption at $283\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed a high adsorption enthalpy of $-48.2\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for MIL-101(Cr) at zero loading. Despite the presence of open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in HKUST-1, a comparatively weaker adsorption enthalpy of $-23.4\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ was found at similar loading. The adsorption enthalpy decreases at an increased loading of about 6 mmol $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $-17.4~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ . The authors justified the higher adsorption enthalpy in MIl-101(Cr), with a stronger electrostatic contribution between ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ and CO. The authors also suggested, that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ cations in HKUST-1 are not fully accessible, because the coordinated solvent molecules from the synthesis were not completely removed.335
F´erey et al. studied the interaction behavior of MIL-100(Fe) and CO in dependence of the activation conditions, (see above coordinated solvent removal strategies).142 In situ IR spectroscopic analysis using CO as a probe allowed both the oxidation states of iron and the amount of OMS to be quantified. At room temperature, CO does not interact strongly with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites (weak band at $2190~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ ). An increase in the activation temperature leads to partial $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ reduction to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and to the appearance of two new bands at 2182 and $2173~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (that are still observed after desorption under vacuum). In the case, where only open $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites are present the heat of adsorption is $-39\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . For the partially reduced sample, where both, open $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites are present the value of the heat of adsorption increases to about $-51\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ . IR measurements revealed, that the higher value confirms the slightly stronger interaction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ cations with CO over that of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations. Here, the weak interaction between the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations and CO are coincident with the weak band at $2190~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The appearance of two CO valence bands at 2182 and $2173~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , was assigned to the adsorption on the open Fe2+.142
## NO adsorption
Open metal sites MOFs were applied for the specific nitric oxide (NO) adsorption and storage in view of the known good ligating properties of nitrosyl towards transition metal atoms. MOF have been investigated to store and to release NO, e.g. by exposure to water in connection to its pharmaceutical effect. NO is a biological signaling molecule, and is used in vitro and in vivo for anti-bacterial, anti-thrombotic and wound healing applications. MOFs are envisioned for the storage, delivery and biological activity of NO for these in healthcare applications. In part, this NO delivery is achieved on exposure of the MOF to moisture.74,90
The interaction of the NO with the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in HKUST-1 and with the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ sites in M-MOF-74 was reported by Morris et al.90,336,337 They compared the NO sorption data of the three OMS-MOFs with MIL-53(Al/Cr) MOFs without OMS. Thereby, HKUST-1 ( $\sim3$ mmol NO per g) and M-MOF-74 $\mathbf{\tilde{M}}=\mathbf{Co}$ , Ni; $\mathord{\sim}7$ mmol NO per g) adsorbed high amounts of NO at RT in comparison to MIL-53 $<1\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ). Moreover, the MOFs HKUST-1 and M-MOF-74 showed also large, and MIL-53 a small hysteresis on desorption of NO (Fig. 50).90 The large hysteresis indicated the expected ligand binding of NO to OMS. HKUST-1 released only $1\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ NO per $\mathrm{g}$ out of the $\sim3$ mmol NO per $\mathrm{g}$ which underscore the NO chemisorption in this MOF. About 2.21 mmol ${\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ is not desorbed when the NO pressure is reduced to almost zero.90 This corresponds to ${\sim}1\mathrm{NO}$ per dicopper SBU in HKUST-1.133,337
In HKUST-1 $\mathrm{{Cu}^{1+}}$ sites can be formed via TA or photoreduction (see Section 2). The reduced open $\mathrm{{Cu}^{1+}}$ sites in HKUST-1 interact more strongly with NO in the presence of water, compared to the interaction of the higher oxidation state $(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ with NO.338,339
The nature of this $\mathbf{Cu-NO}$ interactions was investigated by XPS and NEXAFS with the latter showing a decrease of the intensity of the $\mathrm{{Cu}^{1+}}$ peak and an increase of the area of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ peak with the introduction of NO (Fig. 51). This suggest that $\mathrm{Cu^{1+}}$ was oxidized by NO to form $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{NO}^{-}$ . The reformed ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centers are then different (due to the bound NO) than the pristine ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in HKUST-1. $^{338,339}\mathrm{Zn\mathrm{-}M O F\mathrm{-}74}^{340}$ adsorbed approx. 5 mmol NO per g.336 Hartmann and his co-workers341 investigated the spectroscopic properties of NO adsorbed in M-MOF-74 $\bf(M=\bf C o^{2+},\ N i^{2+})$ , by using low-temperature electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) and IR spectroscopy. IR spectra showed the stretching frequency of NO $\left(1878\ \mathrm{\cm}^{-1}\right.$ for free NO) for Ni-NO at $1838~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and for Co–NO adducts at $1797\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . These two bands were retained upon desorption at RT due to the good ligating action of NO with Co and Ni, again as expected from the good ligating action of NO towards transition metals.341
## $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ sorption
Safe and efficient storage and separation of acetylene $\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)$ is a major challenge. Since highly reactive acetylenes explode at room temperature above a pressure of $2\ \mathrm{atm}$ , the industrial focus is on the development of low-pressure acetylene storage and separation methods.342 MOFs provide an alternative solution for storing and depositing $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at low pressures.343 Especially MOFs with OMS could make an important contribution to $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ storage technology.261,262,344–351
OMS–MOFs show high values for the isosteric heats of adsorption for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , from approx. $-30.4$ to $-39.0\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for HKUST-1, $-41.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Mg-MOF-74, $-45.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Co-MOF-74 to $-45.0$ to $-46.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for Fe-MOF-74 with the highest $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value.209,261,262,351 Comparatively, the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpies for MOFs without OMS such as MIL-53, MOF-5 and ZIF-8 are in the range of approx. 13 to 20 kJ mol1.262
Compared to non-OMS MOFs, Chen et al. attributed the $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ value for HKUST-1 to the interaction of the $\mathbf{Cu}^{2+}\mathbf{-OMS}$ with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . The authors established the preferred interaction site between deuterated acetylene molecules and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ by neutron powder diffraction studies. Rietveld refinement of the neutron diffraction data revealed, that at a $0.62\mathrm{~C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu loading acetylene is adsorbed entirely at an open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site. A linear orientation of the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule parallel to the O–Cu–O axis was found, with a Cu–C bonding distance of $2.62\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ . Adsorption at the entrance window of a small cage (cage window site), was found at an increased loading of $1.5\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu. The authors concluded that these primary and secondary adsorption sites together cause the relatively high acetylene uptake of $201\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ for HKUST-1 at RT and 1 atm.262
## $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ adsorption
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ has technical relevance as the main component of natural gas. Increasing the storage capacity in a given volume or at a given pressure by adsorption in a (cheap and stable) porous material would be highly attractive. Referring to this, MOFs have been extensively studied and have been proven to be promising materials for methane storage.352–357 A promising approach to increase the methane storage capacity was seen in the design of MOFs with suitable pore sizes and a high density of open metal sites, which can more strongly interact with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ than the organic pore walls.358–360
In previous studies authors claim an influence of OMS in connection with increased adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .361 Within the M-MOF-74 series, it was found that Ni-MOF-74 exhibits the highest $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ at low $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ loading and adsorbs the largest amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . According to neutron diffraction experiments, the OMS were identified as primary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption sites (Fig. 52).294 Physisorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ on the OMS in Mg-MOF-74, was confirmed using neutron diffraction experiments with the experimental $\mathbf{Mg\cdot\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ distance of $3.4{\overset{\circ}{\operatorname{A}}}.$ . This distance is only slightly smaller than the experimental $\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\cdot{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ distance of $3.45\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorbed on a graphite surface. In Mg-MOF-74 the neighboring methane molecules on sites I and II have a C C distance of $\sim3.73\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , only slightly smaller than that in solid methane, suggesting that the adsorbed $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules on site II has similar weak interactions with both the framework (mainly the C and O atoms on the organic linker) and the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules on site I (Fig. 52).294
However, also in this work, the heats of adsorption for M-MOF-74 $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+},\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+},\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{2+},\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{2+},\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}^{2+})$ ), delivered from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms (at 270, 280, and 298 K), are in the range of approx. $-18.3\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ to $-20.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , with little variation with the metal and no variation with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ loading (Fig. 53, left).294 It must be noted that the absence of higher $Q_{\mathrm{st}}^{0}$ values at low loading speaks against an effect or significant interaction with OMS.
At the same time, the authors added, that in the case of methane, the $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\cdot{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ distance is largely constrained by the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ geometry (a steric effect) and thus varies very little among different metals. This makes the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ binding strength nearly independent of the open metal species. Therefore, we conclude that different from other adsorbates with dipole or quadrupole moment, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ interacts only very weakly with OMS and not much stronger than with the organic pore walls.
Different to the finding of Yildirim et al., where the methane heat of adsorption was almost constant for the whole loading range, Blom et al. highlighted a decrease from $^{-20}$ to $-22\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ to approx. $-18.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ upon full occupancy for both Ni-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74 (Fig. 53, right). The heat of adsorption was calculated from the isotherms at $179\mathrm{~K~}$ and $283\mathrm{~K~}$ .321
Zhou et al. predicted for the paddle-wheel MOFs, HKUST-1, PCN-11 and PCN-14, that direct binding of a methane molecule to any ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site should be possible at room temperature and 35 bar, but this would then represent only a fraction of the total storage capacity in these MOFs.358 The adsorption enthalpy for UTSA-20 from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms at 200 K, 240 K, $270~\mathrm{K}$ , and $300\mathrm{~K~}$ , was calculated to be $-17.7\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at zero coverage.360 We note that it was not elucidated further if OMS had an influence on the heat of adsorption and if the heat of adsorption decreased after OMS coverage. The experimental determined heats of adsorption of $-17.1\mathrm{\AA}[5]\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for HKUST-1 and $-17.5(5)\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ for $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}],$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption isotherms at room temperature and 35 bar are in a similar range.264 In this experimental work, Brown et al. found for $[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}^{2+},\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}^{2+})$ , that at $\mathrm{CD}_{4}$ loadings of 0.4, 0.7, the primary adsorption sites are around and within the small octahedral cage of the network and these adsorptions sites are favored over the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ sites, as assessed by situ neutron powder diffraction measurements. Additional nuclear density in the octahedral cages and at the OMS was discovered, both for HKUST-1 and for $[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}]$ at an increased loading ${\mathrm{7.5~CD}}_{4}$ per metal atom).264
## $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ adsorption
Sulfur dioxide $\left(\mathbf{SO}_{2}\right)$ is an acidic and toxic gas in the atmosphere, which originates from both natural and anthropogenic source. The burning of fossil fuels and industrial processes contribute to the anthropogenic emissions. Although flue gas desulfurization (FGD) is in large parts $(85-95\%)$ carried out with established lime-stone absorption,362,363 up to $150{-}450~\mathrm{ppm}$ of residual $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ are emitted to the atmosphere, contributing a large part to the $80~\mathrm{{Mt}}$ of worldwide anthropogenic $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ emissions from energy related sources in 2015.364 New materials and processes to enhance FGD are desirable.365–367 Open metal sites can act as sites for $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ adsorption.368,369 In 2008, Yaghi et al.79 reported on the dynamic adsorption capacity of e.g. HKUST-1 and Zn-MOF-74, which are potential OMS-MOFs.
The adsorption of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ to open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites was reported for the paddle-wheel MOF MFM-170.122 In situ synchrotron single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments show the $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ coordination to OMS within a MOF for the first time. The preferred $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ adsorption site was found on a three-fold rotational axis in the triangular window of metal–organic cuboctahedra. The open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites represent the secondary adsorption sites, where the $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ molecule binds to the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site in an end-on mode $\left[\mathbf{O}(\mathrm{SO_{2}})–\mathbf{Cu}=2.28\AA(10)~\mathrm{\AA}\right]$ with an occupancy of 0.67 (Fig. 54).
The authors showed via controlled desorption of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ from MFM-170 that the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site is the most thermodynamically favored site. The diffraction data was collected under a dynamic vacuum at $298\mathrm{K}$ and desorption of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ left the last adsorbate as the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -bound $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ with an occupancy of 0.09. This suggests that the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site is the thermodynamically strongest binding site, but (due to the Jahn–Teller effect) is still weak so that it is almost entirely desorbed on reduction of pressure.122
## Separation
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis and breakthrough experiments have been performed to investigate the role of OMS on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}$ or $\mathrm{CO_{2}/C H_{4}}$ gas separation process.178,378
It has been shown that especially Mg-MOF-74 possesses high selectivity for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , since the main-group metal $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ has no strong preference for CO (see above).71,178 In breakthrough experiments with Mg-MOF-74 and a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}$ gas mixture a longer breakthrough time for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ than for CO was found, resulting in a high separation ability of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over CO.178
Furthermore, Mg-MOF-74 exhibits a high selectivity of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .71 Yaghi et al. showed that when Mg-MOF-74 is exposed to a gas mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in a ratio of 1 : 5, it only adsorbs $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . Breakthrough data revealed an $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $8.9\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ corresponding to 0.44 molecules of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ per $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ site before breakthrough. In comparison, for Zn-MOF-74, a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake of only $0.35~\mathrm{wt\%}$ was obtained under the same conditions.71
Schro¨der et al. have recently reported the coordination of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ to open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in MFM-170. MFM-170 provides high selectivity for $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ .122 The authors calculated the selectivity for equimolar mixtures of $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}$ and $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from single-component isotherms at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar. Based on ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST), high selectivity was proposed for $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{N}_{2}$ (944), followed by $\mathrm{SO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (260), $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}$ (203) and $\mathbf{SO}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ (35).122
Calero et al. noted, that among the OMS-MOFs Fe-MOF-74 shows the largest adsorption selectivity values for both $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ gas mixtures at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and low pressure.351
## Catalysis and photocatalysis
The utilization of heterogeneous MOF catalysts can offer a number of advantages over homogeneous metal catalysts, including easy separation, efficient recycling, minimization of metal traces in the product, and improved handling and process control. MOF catalysts also aim to exploit porosity and chemical tunability for reaction selectivity.32,39 There are several strategies to implement catalytic activity in the MOFs structure. Catalytic sites can be incorporated into the bridging ligands or postsynthetically grafted onto the linker or the metal SBU of the MOF. Often MOF catalysts try to use their OMS as catalytically active centers. After formation, the OMS will act as a Lewis acid, and it will be available to accept electron density from any donor molecule that can be present.379
We note that there is a myriad of literature on MOF catalysis claiming the influence of OMS without proof. We did not include and will not comment this work here but have limited the noted work to cases with proven OMS effects. Such examples describing the use of MOF catalysts containing OMS have been published, with uses in the cyanosilylation of carbonyl compounds380 or of benzaldehyde or acetone,125 the Mukaiyama aldol condensation,381 the organophosphorous ester hydrolysis,382 the cycloaddition with propylene epoxide and other small substrates,383 the isomerization of $\mathfrak{a}$ -pinene oxide384 or of glucose,385 the conversion of citronellal into isopulegol,384 as well as for the oxidation of alcohols39,91 or various organic substrates99 or for selective hydrogenations38,153 and in photocatalysis.386
In 1994, Fujita et al.387 demonstrated for the first time the utility of a 2D MOF $[\mathrm{Cd}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}]$ , (bpy $\c=$ bipyridine) as a heterogeneous Lewis acid catalyst, which promoted the cyanosilylation of aldehydes with shape selectivity. Kaskel et al.125 showed that the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in HKUST-1 could catalyze the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde or acetone. De Vos et al.384 reported that activated HKUST-1 is a highly selective Lewis acid catalyst for the isomerization of $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -pinene oxide to campholenic aldehyde, the cyclization of citronellal to isopulegol, and the rearrangement of $\mathfrak{a}$ -bromoacetals due to its free ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ coordination sites. Long et al.381 described Mn-btt for the cyanosilylation of aromatic aldehydes with good conversion for small substrates and also substrate-size selectivity. Mn-btt can also be used to catalyze the Mukaiyama aldol reaction. The Lewis acid $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites in the paddle-wheel MOF $\big[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\big[\big(\mathrm{C}_{20}\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\big)\big(\mathrm{COO}\big)_{4}\big]\big],$ with accessible acylamide groups383 is a heterogeneous catalyst for carbon fixation via catalytic conversion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .
The OMS MOF MIL-101(Cr) was reported by Kaskel et al.380 for the cyanosilylation of carbonyl compounds. The authors demonstrated that MIL-101(Cr) with open $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites possess a higher Lewis acidity to yield a more active catalyst for the cyanosilylation reaction, in comparison to HKUST-1. OMSMIL-101(Cr) showed a high yield of $98.5\%$ at $313\mathrm{~K~}$ after $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the catalytic sites were immune to unwanted reduction by benzaldehyde.
Li et al.39 generated a $\mathbf{Pd/MIL-101(Cr)}$ catalyst for the liquidphase aerobic oxidation of various alcohols and analyzed the effect of OMS of the MOF-support on the reactivity of Pd nanoparticles for the alcohol oxidation. It was observed that after ethylenediamine grafting on the open ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites the catalytic Pd activity was suppressed. The oxidation of cinnamyl alcohol indicated results that range from $<3/98$ to 99/99 $\left(\mathrm{conv.}/\mathrm{sel\%}\right)$ in the order $\mathrm{{ML}101(C r)\ <\ 0.35\%}$ $\begin{array}{l l l}{\mathrm{Pd/En-MIL-101(Cr)}}&{<}&{0.35\%}\end{array}$ Pd/MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ . Accordingly, the use of $0.35\%\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ gave a complete conversion of cinnamyl alcohol to cinnamyl aldehyde within $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ at $80\ {}^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ while the amine grafted sample $0.35\%$ Pd/En-MIL-101 showed only $45\%$ yield of cinnamyl aldehyde within $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After addition of a small amount of base additives (e.g. NaOH) to the system the activity increased ( $95\%$ conversion of alcohol within $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ ). It can be deduced that the open $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ sites play an imported role in the alcohol oxidation related to a promoting effect observed in MIL-101-supported metal nanoparticles for the conversions of aromatics.388,389
The MOFs TMU-10 and 12 with open ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites were studied by Morsali et al.390 for the oxidative desulfurization reaction of a model oil, which was prepared by dissolving dibenzothiophene (DBT) in $n$ -hexane. The Lewis acidic ${\bf C o}^{2+}$ sites in these structures successfully completed the quantitative desulfurization process after $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Kitagawa et al.385 investigated the potential of MIL-101(Cr) derivatives for glucose isomerisation. In comparison to Amberlyst-15, a strongly acidic ion-exchange resin, MIL-100 and MIL-101 showed higher catalytic activity. Here, the catalytic activity can be modulated through the Lewis-acidity of the OMS by modification of the organic ligands with functional groups, like $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ , $\mathbf{-}(\mathbf{CH}_{3})_{2}$ , $-\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ . Electron donating $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{H}}_{2}$ and – $\left.-(\mathbf{CH}_{3})_{2}\right.$ groups on the aromatic ring of the terephthalate, decrease the Lewis acidity of open $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites and increase the electron density. Whereas electron-accepting $-\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups increase the Lewis acidity of OMS and decrease the electron density. The authors described also that MIL-100 and MIL-101 have though the same Lewis acidity, but different production yields of fructose, due to the differences in window size of the mesopores. $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ provided a high conversion of glucose and selectively produced fructose.
Hatton et al.382 investigated the modification of MIL-101(Cr) with dialkylaminopyridines (DAAP) for the hydrolytic degradation of organophosphorus esters such as diethyl-4-nitrophenyl phosphate (paraoxon). Different supernucleophilic DAAP ligands were coordinatively attaching onto part of the OMS of MIL-101. The authors showed, that the catalytic activity of the DAAPmodified MIL-101(Cr) was higher than activated MIL-101(Cr). Furthermore, the synergistic activation of the substrate by the Lewis acid $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites and the electron-rich nucleophiles (DAAP) gave a Lewis acid–Lewis base activation mechanism.
For the selective semihydrogenation Luo et al. and coworkers38 reported on Pd@Zn-MOF-74, which showed a high styrene selectivity of $92\%$ at full conversion with a turnover frequency of $98.1\ \mathrm{{h}}^{-1}$ – compared to the selectivity of the Lindlar catalyst with $56\%$ . The origin of ultrahigh selectivity, as proposed by DFT calculation, is due to a coordination of the $\mathbf{C}{\equiv}\mathbf{C}$ bond of phenylacetylene to the open $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ site.38
MIL-101(Fe) was studied for the photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reaction by Li et al.386 The authors demonstrated with in situ FT-IR studies that the open Fe sites are the photocatalytically active sites for this reaction. The adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ directly onto the metal center activates the substrate for the photocatalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction.
Morsali et al.391 have reported the mixed-metal MOFs with open Cd sites for phenol degradation from aqueous solutions under UV and/or visible light irradiation. The authors compared the four TMU MOFs, $[\mathrm{Zn(oba)(4\cdotbpdh)_{0.5}}],$ $[\mathrm{Cd}_{0.15}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.85}(\mathrm{oba})(4\mathrm{-bpdh})_{0.5}],$ $[\mathrm{Cd}_{0.3}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.7}(\mathrm{oba})(4\mathrm{-bpdh})_{0.5}]$ and $[\mathrm{Cd(oba)}(4{\cdot}\mathrm{bpdh)}]$ . The mixed metal-based MOFs with 15 and $30\%$ Cd were photocatalytically more active than the just Cd or Zn containing MOF.
Hong et al.111 explained that the activation temperature had an important effect on the catalytic activity for CO oxidation because more open metal sites available in HKUST-1 gave a higher CO activity, the catalytic activity then increased in the order HKUST $-1-553\mathrm{~K~<~}443\mathrm{~K~}473\mathrm{~K~<~}503\mathrm{~K~<~}523\mathrm{~K~}$
## Sensing
Chemical materials for highly selective and sensitive detection of analytes in the gas and liquid phase are competitively investigated for applications in industrial processes, chemical threat detection, medical diagnostics, food quality control, occupational safety and environmental monitoring. Thereby Lewis acidic or basic sites in the ligands or OMS play an important role through their interactions with guest molecules for the selective recognition. Often luminescent MOFs (LMOFs) are utilized as sensor through the quenching or enhancement of their luminescence by the analyte. Mesoporous LMOFs allow also the sensing of larger molecules such as biologically active species.392
A few examples of porous MOFs with open metal sites have been investigated for their sensing properties. Morsali et al.393 showed the potential of open $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ sites in activated TMU-41, $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)_{1.5}(L)]}$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{L}}=\mathbf{\bar{\Phi}}$ pyridine 4-carboxylate) for the selective and recyclable luminescent sensing of $\mathbf{MnO_{4}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}{\bf O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{CrO_{4}}^{2-}$ . Lobkovsky et al.180 showed that the luminescent MOF $\big[\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\big]$ after removal of the solvent molecules contained open ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ sites, which could act as a highly selective sensor for small molecules, such as dimethyl formamide, acetone, and ethanol. For more practical use weakly coordinated 1-propanol molecules on the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ sites were replaced by DMF and acetone to gradually increase and decrease the fluorescence intensity, respectively.
The lanthanide MOF $\big[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}\big(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-pzdc}\big)\big(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-pzdc}\big)\big(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-ox}\big)\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{4}\big]$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pzdc}\ =\ 2,5\right.$ -pyrazinedicarboxylic acid, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{ox}=$ oxalic acid) was reported by Guillou et al.394 for the recognition and sensing of acetone. After removal of the solvent molecules the MOF exhibited a high quenching effect with acetone.
Chen et al.395 reported on the near infrared (NIR) luminescent Yb-MOF $\left[\mathrm{Yb(bpt)(H_{2}O)(D M F)_{1.5}(H_{2}O)_{1.25}}\right]$ (bpt $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ biphenyl-3,4,5- tricarboxylate) for the sensing of small molecules. Activated $\left[\mathrm{Yb(bpt)}\right]$ showed typical NIR emission from the $^{2}\mathrm{F}_{5/2}\rightarrow^{2}\mathrm{F}_{7/2}$ transition of ${\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}$ at $980~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , when excited at $326~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ . In comparison to $\left[\mathrm{Eu(btc)}\right]$ the luminescence intensity of $\left[\mathrm{Yb(bpt)}\right]$ indicated even higher enhancing and quenching effects with DMF and acetone.
Dinca˘ et $a l.^{41}$ described the interaction of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with $\left[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(t c p e)}\right]$ (tcpe $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)ethylene) and $\mathrm{[Mg(H_{2}d h b d c)]}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dhbdc}^{2-}=2,5\right.$ -dihyroxybenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate), which caused fluorescence shifts and turn-on luminescence responses over potential interferents such as water, methanol, amines, and other gases. The exposure of activated $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(t c p e)]}$ to ammonia, triethylamine, ethylenediamine, $N{\mathrm{,}}N{\mathrm{-}}$ -diethylformamide and water vapors shifted its emission maximum by up to $23\mathrm{nm}$ at room temperature. At $100~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ ammonia exposure led to a shift of the emission maximum from 487 to $511~\mathrm{nm}$ , thus revealing a good selectivity for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ detection. Based on in situ IR studies the authors verified the binding of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ to the open $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ sites in $\mathrm{[Mg(H_{2}d h b d c)]}$ .
## 5. Conclusions
The as-synthesized MOF will have initially all metal ions in its SBU in their fully coordinatively saturated state. This is the most stable metal coordination environment according to the usual metal coordination number, which is typically six (octahedral) or four (tetrahedral) for most 3d transition metal ions. Open metal sites in MOFs (OMS-MOFs) can be obtained by the removal of an initial labile terminal ligand, which may typically be a solvent molecule, from its coordinating metal atom. The procedure for this ligand removal with OMS generation is usually solvent exchange and removal, which may be generally termed ‘‘activation’’ and can be further differentiated into thermal, chemical or photothermal activation. Seldom noted, but definitely more important, is the at least partial reduction of the metal at the OMS upon its formation, as was already observed for HKUST-1 with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}$ and MIL-100 with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ; but such a reduction may also occur in many other OMS-MOFs.
OMS are often invoked but it is important to prove the presence of OMS by suitable analytical methods, such as NMR, TGA, FT-IR in connection with gas sorption studies. It is just becoming apparent that quantitative OMS generation cannot be taken for granted by ‘‘standard’’ activation protocols. Few studies have so far addressed the issue of OMS quantification and more such studies are needed to quantify the formed OMS.
The current characterization techniques for the detection and quantification of Lewis-acid sites in MOFs are gas or vapor adsorption, probe-molecule infrared spectroscopy and temperatureprogrammed desorption (TPD). The techniques are versatile but their application in OMS research remains limited. Most of the OMS work simply did not address the quantities of OMS formed. Also, further improvement and additional techniques are warranted. The ${\bf N H}_{3}$ -TPD technique, for example, has the limitation in quantifying OMS in the presence of Brønsted acid sites.
In MOF structures any change to the metal ions in the metal SBU must ensure the integrity of the network. Importantly, the MOF structure must not collapse, and their crystallinity and porosity should be preserved, that is, a labile terminal ligand should be removed without damage to the framework. Retention of the framework integrity is usually verified by PXRD.
It is the strength of theory that OMS can be easily studied regarding their electronic effects on the interactions with substrate molecules. The theory-based knowledge, how OMS interact with the guest molecules is needed along the experimental work for understanding the properties of OMS-MOFs. We note, however, that without additional parametrization, standard force fields are barely able to deliver accurate thermodynamic data. More accurate values for binding enthalpies are obtained through quantum mechanical calculation of potential energy surfaces, preferably using periodic boundaries. At the DFT-level, the inclusion of van der Waals interactions is crucial. The most accurate approach involves a combination of dispersion corrected DFT and high-level ab initio methods, as e.g. DFT-D and coupled cluster (CC) methods including complete basis set extrapolation. Theoretical studies support that OMS interactions with gaseous adsorbates are dispersiondriven for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , involve electrostatic and dispersion for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , and contain a partially covalent (dative) bonding character with CO and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ .
The presence of OMS in the MOF causes enhanced interactions between the metal site and guest molecules. OMS are the primary adsorption sites for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , CO, NO and $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ giving high heats of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ . OMS are, however, not the primary adsorption site for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and possibly $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ . The formation of an OMS is also the prerequisite for the ‘‘metal-binding’’ of weakly coordinating adsorbates such as $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . Gas sorption studies on OMS-MOFs in connection with high-resolution/powder neutron diffraction studies, powder synchrotron X-ray diffraction analysis and near sites edge X-ray adsorption fine structure (NEXAFS) spectroscopy yielded structural information and insight into the role of the OMS on the MOF–adsorbate interactions.
The defined synthesis of OMS in MOFs is the basis for targeted functionalization through grafting, the coordination of weakly binding species and increased (supramolecular) interactions with guest molecules. The enhanced interactions can be highly effective on the MOF performance in the potential applications gas sorption and separation, catalysis and sensing. For example, MOFs with OMS are among the most promising solid adsorbents for selectively removing $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ from dry gas mixtures and have been described as highly active Lewis-acid catalysts.
